 . _ .. _</p>
        <p>the ages of ssix and ten years, the Guild undertaking that they are healthy, clean, deserving, and each will have a new outfit of presentable clothing before doing on their visit. Many of course already do much in their own locality to provide Christmas treats for the children of the poor, but this scheme is intended to reach as many as possible of those who would other- wise stand no chance of tasting the good things, and happiness of Christmastide. I Infantile Fashions.—ln the matter of quite small girls' party frocks, the long waist with the• very short petticoat is still the effect most sought after. Point ! d'Esprit, crystalline, nun's veiling, and soft silk, inlet with Valenciennes lace, are the fabrics preferred, the • point d'esprit coming first in favour. The majority of mothers prefer that the silk slip as well as the over- dress, should be white depending for the necessary dash •of colour upon the sash, shoes, and hair ribbon. Fringe is again correctfor the little folks, which is a move in the right direction, as the average child looks very much prettier with softly falling hair, cut neatly across the • forehead. Mittens are donned by quite tiny girls, and a little plain gold bangle, from which some charm , depends, is deemed permissible by fashionable mothers I*- Concerning Boy's Dress.—For little boys of from • four to nine or ten 4 years of age, the sailor suit for day ' wear stands easily first, then comes the knickerbocker suit with a jacket, and plain soft-fronted shirt. Any- thing in the tform of an art coloured plush costume, accompanied by a lace collar, and falling curls, is abso- lutely out of date, the present ide., . apparently being to make little men of the youngsttirs, and not pictures ; The overcoats are cut on the same lines as those of their fathers, and their hats and gloves,and soft fronted • shirts, are the same on a reduced scale. The Princess of Wales has always preferred the sailor suit, in which the young Princes invariably'appear. Her Royal High- ness too has always favoured the greatest simplicity t where her children's dress is concerned, and with the exception of a few American women, and some of the "newly rich," Society, fmothers generally have been quick to follow ths Princess's example. , Dry Cleaning.—The difficulty of getting ones garments back from the dry cleaners under two or three . weeks, deters many women from wearing white, cream or light-coloured toilettes in the winter. but it would seem 'as though this state of things is on the eve of improvement. The Parisienne has rarely to waitlonger than two or three days for her blouses, costumes, hats, &amp;c., and some London firms now announce that customers may depend upon a similar prompt delivery of goods sent to be dry cleaned. A white or cream coat and skirt, under these altered circumstances, will be a possi- bility. for many women, and with a touch of colour about the neck, headgear, and underskirt frills, such a scheme takes a great deal of beating.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
