      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WELSH SAILOR DROWYED AT R UNCORN</head>
        <p>WELSH SAILOR DROWYED AT R UNCORN</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, about 11 p.m. on Sunday, William Evans, aged sixty, of New Quay, North Wales, was drowned in the Alfred Dock. Evans was mate of the schooner William Gilmore. which is in the sale list. The whole of the crew were paid off on Saturday, but the deceased was gi n the privilege of sleeping on board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>. . ASK YOUR GROCER FOR McDOUGALL'S Pent SELF-RAISING FLOUF tho, Examiner, SaturdaY, December 1/, 1904. ii Fitstilms tiF TO-DAY• "ENGLAND3S. CURSE."</p>
        <p>hour of victory c C)113 it mast,they wx.11,1 hv the glal consciousness of knowing that in this fight between Heaven and hell they had struck hard and helped to wipe away the tears from the face of a so:rowing world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>REV.. V. MINNIE° AND THZ EVILS OF INTEMPERANCE. "The Chief Cause of Bad Trade and ' Lack of Employment"</head>
        <p>THE REV.. V. MINNIE° AND THZ EVILS OF INTEMPERANCE. "The Chief Cause of Bad Trade and ' Lack of Employment"</p>
        <p>THE CONDUCT OF POLITICAL CLUBS.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was again crowded on Sunday evening when the Rev. Herbert Dunnico delivered a serraou on "England's Curse." Basing his discourse on the words "Every tree whicda my Path&amp; hath not planted shall be uprooted" the ;preacher said that the subject he had chosen was not only a vast one but also very complex. He candidly admitted that he hardly knew where to begin or what to say. Possibly some of his congrega- tion would go home feeling that he was hard or narrow or bigoted but if that happened he could not help it. Perhaps that -was the inevitable penalty, of plainly speaking one's convictions. Still he hoped they would accept his assurance that no word of his was intended to hurt the feelings of any one individual. To him the drink traffic as such was ' England's greatest I curse. It was the mother of poverty and thej nurse of crime. It ruined more houses, wrecked more I bodies, degraded more souls than anything else andj ought to be cursed by all men. Looking at the "trade" from a social standpoint no one could deny its evil effects. He questioned very seriously whether there was a single person:in that vast gathering who could say that drink bad not either directly or indirectly marred his happiness. Thousands of houses in this land were at this time being ruined by it and many a father and mother were being sent with grey hairs and broken hearts to their grave. A few years ago the body of a young man was found in the Mersey near Liverpool. ' In his pocket was a paper beating these words "This is ' the end of a wasted life. Let me rot. Do not ask my name. Drink did it." In a very few days the Coroner of Liverpool received more than 200 letters from parents I in various parts of the country asking if there were any , signs by which the body could be identified since they I had lost their son. He was somebody's son, and he (the preacher) put it to them in this form. Supposing that lad had been their lad, the pride of their life, the joy of their home,the prop of their old age and he had fallen a I victim to drink. Would they smile disdainfully, would they call him a narrow bigoted fanatic if he denounced this stuff as he did denounce it as the fruit of hell. Let them go into their police courts and hear there the tales of woe. They did not like to hear them, perhaps, ; neither did he. But the way not to hear them was not to ignore them but to remove that which made them possible. IWeek by week and day by day women were being deser- ted and abused by drunken husbands. Mothers were I forsaken and even beaten by drunken sons ; little chil- dren were left in dirt and squalor to suffer,to starve, to steal and to become the thieves and felons and harlots of the future. It was not in police courts and slums and streets that one could see the whole or even the chief results of the trade. Its influence was felt through-, out Society, frustrating the free development of the noblest manhood and womanhood and violating the high- est and holiestsanctities of the home. In the industrial world the effects of the drink trade were literally in. calculable. Not long ago he had</p>
        <p>COME INTO CONFLICT WITH 'A CERTAIN SECTION OF SOCIALISTS</p>
        <p>because he had ventured to assert that drink was the greatest evil of the industrial world. Some of them had devoted hours so try and convince the people that poverty was the cause and not the result of drunkenness. Some had gone so far as to say that thp economic con- ditions of the industrial classes would not be one whit better if they abandoned their wasteful indulgence in strong drink. Such a contention was extremely foolish. If poverty was the cause of drunkenness, how was it that the middle and upper classes who lived in luxury and all that could be desired spent 2i to 3 times as much per head upon drink as the poor working man. The effects of drink among the upper classes were not so apparent as among the poorer for obvious reasons. The prospezous, well-fed and comfortable classes were able to counteract its effects by rest and change. Another thing, it was a well-known fact that a general increase of wages meant an increase of drunkenness and a year of prosperity was always marked by a rise of general drunkenness and an increase of insanity and criminal disorder. An ex-President of the Trades Union Congress has stated that the working-man had more to fear from drinking and gambling than from all the capitalists put together. They were being told by certain politicians that England was on the decline, and that the foreigner was taking their trade. Personally, he did not' share those views, but if these men were right—and many of them were able men—and our com- mercial supremacy WAS in danger the worst of all possible ways to meet it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TO PANDER TO AND ENDOW</head>
        <p>TO PANDER TO AND ENDOW</p>
        <p>that which was paralizing the hand and muddling the brain of the British working man. One of the largest employers of labour in the North of England had stated that out of 800 men in his employment 400 lost several days a week through their drinking habits and even when they came their powers were greatly impaired. It is estimated that at least one-sixth of the productive power of this great nation is lost through drink. Let them look at thel wages sheet of any Brewery or Dis- tillery and compare it with that of any other trade and they would be astounded. Where an ordinary busi- ness would pay £130,000 in wages a brewery company would pay between £5,000 and £6,000. For every £lOO spent in coal, £55 was paid in wages. For every £lOO spent in intoxicants, gi only was paid in wages, There was in Dublin a brewery with a capital of £14,000,000 which found employment for 3000 people, In the Linen Industry of Belfast with a similar capital of £14,800,000 employment was found forloo,ooo hands. If the capital invested in this trade were deverted into the proper channels of industry</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF THE UNEMPLOYED</p>
        <p>would be practically solved. Drunkenness was the chief cause of bad trade and lack of employment it was largely the cause and not the effect of poverty; it involved the maximum expenditure to the consumers and afforded the mimimum employment to the producer The labour employed was worse than wasted because it resulted in loss to consumer and mischief to the com- munity. It was foolish for any man to stand up and pose as a social reformer unless he was prepared to start at the root and reform the drink traffic. This would•lead the masses of this country from the dark bondage of drinking and squalid poverty to the promised land of moral and industrial happiness. Dealing with his subject from the political point of view he said that the trade was</p>
        <p>THE GREATEST POLITICAL MENACE ,we had to contend against. The "Trade" was fast ! becoming a political ring-which threatened to control ; the commonwealth. The "Trade" was better organized than either of the great political parties. The motto was "Our trade, our politics." It would support any man irrespective of partylavourable to it. At the present time 470 members of the House of Commons , were pledged to defend its interests. English political !life was fast becoming as corrupt as that of America ! where the most powerful political factor was the saloon. ' He did not hesitate to say that if politics were to become as they were becoming the battlefield of privileged interests powerfully organized we were entering upon an era of demoralisation and corruption. Even</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>IN MUNICIPAL LIFE</head>
        <p>IN MUNICIPAL LIFE</p>
        <p>they were feeling the pressure in all this. Would any one venture to deny but that our two local brewery companies exerted far too great an influence in local as well as national politics. The shocking disclosures in Manchester, Liverpool, Derby, Devonport, Notting- ham and Wigan all went to prove his contention. The Chief Constable of Wigan had publicly declared that his hands had:been absolutely tied behind his back by the brewers'on the Watch Committee. The drink was also demoralizing and degrading politics in the shape of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FULLY LICENSED POLITIQAL CLUBS.</head>
        <p>FULLY LICENSED POLITIQAL CLUBS.</p>
        <p>There was not a club in Warrington be it Liberal, Tory or anything else which any self-respecting father would like his son to spend his evenings in. Some clubs were heft-beds of drunkenness and the best of them were ndt paragons of decency. The Independent Labour Party Club at Stalybridge had recently been closed for drunkenness, many disgusting facts being given in evidence at the trial. The primary purpose of these clubs was that of disseminating political know- ledge but he had yet to learn that "booze" and bowls and billiards or id. nap were essential to the production of the noblest type of politician. He warned the political parties that so long as their clubs were carried on as they were so long would the most refined, the most intelligent, the most moral young menremain for the most part outside the party. And if there were young men present who desired to throw themselves as every man ought to throw himself, enthusiastically into politics, but were kept back on account of the things he had mentioned he urged them to meet together to form their own clubs and to demonstrate that the finest politicians and public men could best be produced away from the contaminating influence of drinking and gambling. In conclusion, Mr. Dunnico viewed the subject from the moral and religious point of view. Drink he said was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EXEMY OF RELIGION AND MORALS</head>
        <p>THE EXEMY OF RELIGION AND MORALS</p>
        <p>at home and abroad. The evangelisation of the masses in this land and abroad was impeded by drink as by nothing else. and the gospel had nothing in common with it. The one degraded, the other uplifted, one debased the other restored, one ruined, the other saved. Drink seared the conscience, hardened the heart and dragged the repentant prodigal back again to the sin he fane would leave. Drink enslaved both body and soul, sacrificed our sons on the altar of intemperance and cast our fairest daughters on the streets of our towns and cities to become the sport of filthy men. And yet the State bribed it and the Church of God slumbered and slept when it did not assist. What they had seen be- fore they would ere long see again, the brewer and parson swaggering together to the polling booth. He appealed to them, however,to join a holy crusade against this trade. For hundreds of years it had stalked through the land carrying death and destruction in its train. Still it was 'doomed to fall, Every tree which God had not planted would be uprooted. Let them deter- mine that no . man wha would' Rot pledge himself to resist this trade should represent them in the House of Commons. The fight would not be easy but when the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS A Roman Catholic Instructor for Paclgate Schools.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS A Roman Catholic Instructor for Paclgate Schools.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians met on Wednesday under the presidency of Mr. A. Brooks. There were also present :—Ars. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, the Revs. T. R. Matthews and Dr. Mercer, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Hickman, R. Forshaw, G.. Mather, S. Davies, W. Houghton, G. Thompson, T. H. Ashcroft, J. Payton, W. Flannagan, J. Billinge, T. Ritchie, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (Workhouse master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Schools).</p>
        <p>SCHOOL _,TTENDANCE PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Moore Murray, director of education, wrote as follows: It has been brought to my notice that when children of school age have been discharged from I either the Workhouse or the Padgate schools, they have I often been away from school until they have been found lon the streets by my officers. I should be glad of your assistance in this matter by your asking the responsible ' officers at these institutions to notify me of the depar- ture of any children of school age from the institutions who will reside within the Borough. The CLERK said he had replied that he was quite sues , the Board would supply the information required. t, The Cilarßmax : As this will probably appear in ' the Press I think there should be some explanation that we usually do look after our children. Mr. IVIA.THER: The director of Education wants to be notified when children are discharged from the schools and come back to the town.</p>
        <p>The CaxEII.NIAN : It practically infers tbat the children we have brought up are not looked after. Mr. NIA TIIMR explained that that was not the case. The information was desired in order that when chil- dren were discharged they could immediately commence to attend day schools. At present the school attend- ance officers were unaware when they had returned to the borough.</p>
        <p>SHOULD THE BOARD KEEP HORSES?</p>
        <p>At a meeting of theFflouse Committee Messrs. Jolley and Mather were appointed as sub-committee to enquire and report as to the advisability or otherwise of the Guardians keeping theirjown horses and thus obviate the expense of hiring. The minutes were approved. ROMAN CATHOLIC INSTRUCTOR FOR PIDGATE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Padgate schools Committee on November 29th, it was recommended that the existing arrangements in respect of religious instruction of the children and for their attendance at public worship be revised and that the existing payment as pew rent of £lO to the priest at Woolston Church be continued ; also that the Rev. Father Lowe • be appointed as religious instructor at a salary of £lO a year, andlthat the payment as pew rent in respect of attendance of children It Padgate Church be at the rate of £l5 a year.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON said he wished to renew the appeal he had previously made to the committee in regard to the new arrangements._ _ _</p>
        <p>In moviiig the adoption of the minutes Mr. MATHER said Mr. Houghton;did make a request to the committee but he did not give any reasons for it, although he said there were very good ones. The committee were unanimous in passing the resolution. What Mr. Houghton had to state was of a delicate and personal nature and he (Mr. Mather) believed he did not wish to say it before the reporters. It was the custom at Woolston (R.C.) Church for every individual who attended to pay one penny. Father Lowe asked for no more than that amount in respect of the Padgate school children, ninety-four of whom attended the Church. It was not for the priest's personal benefit as the money went towards the cleaning and repairing of the Church. Mr. JOLLEY seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON said his appeal to the committee was that the minute should be withdrawn for the present. He told the committee that if the question were adjourned, he could say something in committee which would modify the decision arrived at. He wished to avoid any friction and he would again appeal to the committee to withdraw the minute and re-introduce it after due notice. An application was made to the com- mittee in consequence of which they decided to revise the present arrangements. Instead of giving notice of their intention they at once proceeded with the revision which involved the appointment of a new officer and an annual increase in expenditure of £l5. There was, he contended, sufficient warranty for notice of the alter- ations being given. Although he did not differ from the committee in the broad principle of the resolution, he thought he could give reasons for a modification of the decision.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. MERCER said he knew Mr. Houghton was quite in sympathy with the resolution. His objections were personal. Mr. HOUGHTON : Not altogether. The Rev. Dr. MERCER pointed out that there were ninety Catholic children in the homes, but they had not got a single Catholic official to instruct them in their religion—at least there was no Catholic instructor definitely appointed by the committee. Therefore the alternative was that the Board should bring the child- dren up in some other religion than that in which they were entered. That, however, was forbidden by the law. If on the other hand, a child was brought up "as nothing" its conscience would not be formed. If children were brought up without their consciences being. formed in any way, they would in future have their rates multiplied wholesale, because it was not the con- scientioms person who shirked work or rushed to drink and crime and left his family a burden on the rates. It was the non-conscientious person. During the past few years the Board had gone in the right direction to multiply pauperism. He did net think the matter needed much inquiry or consideration to see that it was the duty of the Guardians not to leave ninety children without a religious instructor. He would ask the Board to affirm the principle that a Catholic in- structor be appointed, and, if they wished it, leave the appointment open. In answer to Mr. Hickman, Mr. MATHER said the total number of children in the _schools was 223.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. MERCER: I feel so x.ra—rxiii on the subject that I shall be compelled to press it in every manner I can possibly call to my assistance. The CHAIRMAN said Mr. Houghton was their oldest Guardian and one whose opinion they ought, at all events, to take a little notice of. He had brought a rather, he (the chairman) would not say serious charge, but he told the master that the matter was considered without due notice, and he had kade out a case for postponement. If Mr. Mather instead of pressing the matter would leave open that portion relating to the new religious instructor, the whole thing could be gone into and satisfactorily settled. At present there seemed to be a considerable difference of opinion between gentlemen equally competent to express an opinion. Mr. BILLINGE pointed out that the committee had of the past made some provision for the instruction of the .Roman Catholic children as they had been allowed to attend the services of their church. He was quite prepared to support the minutes to appoint an instructor. He would also be delighted if the funds would permit, to endorse the increase for pew rents in regard to the Church of England children. He did not think the Property Owners' Association would object on those matters. (Laughter.) The Bishop of the diocese might recommend an appointment to the office of Catholic religious instructor for the sanction of the Local Goven- ment Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER said he understood Mr. Houghton acknowledged the principle of paying a Catholic instructor, and the committee could discuss the matter in detail at the next meeting. On the suggestion of Mr. Jolley it was agreed to alter the minutes to the effect that a Roman Catholic religious instructor be appointed, leaving the actual appointment unsettled. Mr. MATHER remarked that the committee were quite unanitn6us in granting the extra 1.5 to Padgate church. The number of childrenattending had increased considerably since the pew rents were arranged, and part of the living came from pew rents alone. The minutes.with the alteration affecting the religious instructor, were confirmed. The matter 'will again be discussed at the first meeting of the committee in the new year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>VACCINATION FEES.</head>
        <p>THE VACCINATION FEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. THOMPSON had given notice of a motion upon the subject of the payment to public vaccinators. The CLERK explained that no Nrrk of the Poor Law Unions Association is being more vigorously pressed than that of opposition to the Order of the Local Government Board fixing the fees to public vaccinators. At the recent annual meeting the Chairman said they had pressed, were pressing, and would continue to press the matter forward without any further motion by the Association. Nevertheless, several speakers (one of whom said that in one case they were paying nearly npao in place of less than £100) to enable the Council to move with more force, insisted upon a further definite resolution which was passed amid great enthusiasm. Mr. THOMPSON said that when he gave notice of motion he felt very strongly in regard to the excessive fees the Board was being charged. In view of the vigorous action of the Poor Law Unions Association he thought it would be best to leave the matter over for the present. He proposed that the Board should pass tt resolution giving the Poor Law Unions Associa- tion warm approval in the steps they were taking. Mr. HotroHrox seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BURNING FATALITIES AT WIDNES</head>
        <p>BURNING FATALITIES AT WIDNES</p>
        <p>Two sad burning fatalities occurred at Widnes last week-end. On Friday afternoon Bessie Davidson, aged four' daughter of Police-constable Davidson, received such severe burns about the hands and face that she died on Sunday at the Accident Hospital. On Saturday morning another child, named Harry Platt, aged four years, son of Peter Platt,• 16, Cross-street, was admitted to the hospital suffering from shocking burns on the face, arms, and legs. His injuries were of so serious a nature that he died on Monday morning.</p>
      </div>
