        <p>TART HAT</p>
        <p>finishes the edge of the A chou of black chiffon with depenC.ing vn an effective note of contrast, a tolich citiic same appear- ing at the elbows in the form of a twist, and a tiny bow above the lace frill. A touch of black is always most effective, but - especially so in connection with a pale toned gown worn by artificial light which it somehow softens and renders' more becoming. This is a fact which is well known to all the leaders of fashiOn, both here and in Paris and.: Vienna, and no doubt is one of the reasons Why the all black hat still holds its. cwn as the. crowning touch to a dressy toile' ~ A coloured hat occasionally jars, er carefully the shades may be chos:,..): the black hat of picturesque outline. 'y trimmed with ostrich feathers or wuc m. and adorned by one long hanthunc f. hcr. can be worn with success with o.!;y tr,,-; and under afl sorts of conditions. My second illustrar: •r. r .'f a ,rt hat of black velvet, with c. in with oyster satin, a cren..ll7 11:1.4 on the bandeau. It i., I.:• white bird whose wing fea:ncr, '';:ar;-:::1-1 tens to almost black. The hiizh cr tvn bas I,:a= of velvet arranged in ban Is round it, and the curved brim bends upwarcl,, at the left side. The result is a charming winter hat which is not too large to adiniz of a veil being worn which is necessary when high winds prevail. Mil- linery is on the whole ,most,,aristic and is also exceedingly varied, so that there should be no</p>
        <p>hew,:</p>
