      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BOLD-STREET CHOIR FgSTIVAL Sermons by the liev. fheodore Bishop</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET CHOIR FgSTIVAL Sermons by the liev. fheodore Bishop</p>
        <p>Gratifyingly successful was the annual choir festival in connection with Bold-street Wesleyan Church which was celebrated on Sunday and Monday. The congrega- tions were large and the collections realised a more sub- stantial sem than for several years past. Special inter- est was lent to Sunday's services by the fact that the preacher was the Rev. Theodore Bishop who, after acting as superintendent of the Warrington circuit, left this town two years ago to take charge of similar work at Llandudno. His many Warrington friends were pleased to welcome him, ani each sermon was listened to with keen interest. A; is usual on such occesions great care had been taken in the arrangement of the musical portions of the services, and the choir earned the highest encomiums for expressive renderings of anthems and choruses. At the morning service the anthems selected were "Send out thy Light" (Gounod) and " Come unto Him" (Gounod), and each was creditably submitted. Miss Mabel Ilensfiall feelingly sang "There is a green hill." Several organ selections by Mr. S. L. Coveney, F.R.C.0., of Dearnley, were given masterly style. The church organist (Mr. Frank Hickman) who had been largely responsible for the training of the choir supplied the organ accompaniments to the anthems and hymns with his customary skill. The subject of the Rev. T. Bishop's sermon was "The Kingdom within" and he based his observations on the text "The kingdom of God is within you," Luke xvii., 21. He pointed out that life, whetherorganic or inorganic, was a great mystery, but the mystery ot the Christian life was even greater. He exhorted his hearers to a trusting faith and said that if a man lost faith he resumed the shackles of sin and thereby blurred the image of God in his soul and lost the consciousness of divine favour and forgiveness. Many men thought when they wanted to better their Christian lives, that they must commence by a more regular attendance at places of worship. According to the teaching of our Lord that was the effect and not the cause, for the kingdom, as He said, was within them. Every man had the making of his own religious life, and the petition "Thy kingdom come" meant the entrance of the' kingdom into the heart of the individual. It should be the aim of Christians to work so that their lives should be according to the ptttern which God had seen it was possible for them to attain. The growing kingdom in the heart meant the entire subjecting of the man in his life, aims, and affections to God and to His service. The perfect kingdom which they sometimes called entire sanctification was" The humble, gentle, patient love of God and our neighbour, ruling our tempers, words, and actions." In the afternoon a musical service was held, Mr. James Dodds presiding over a full attendance. Messrs. Crosfield's prize brass band was in attendance and played severaliselections, including, the test piece in the recent Crystal Palace contest in highly credit- able style. Mr. S. L. Coveney ably contributed a number of organ selections, which formed a most en- joyable feature of the service. Mrs. Arthur Booth very, sweetly and effectively sang " Come unto me ye weary " (Batten). The Chairman, in a short speech, compli- mented Messrs. Crosfield's band upon their splendid victory in the national contest, and also expressed the pleasure it afforded him to meet Mr. Bishop again. At the evening service the choir gave several selec- tions fro&amp; Mealelssohn's" Hymn of praise " the solos being well interpreted by Mrs. Booth, Miss E. Taylor, and Mr. L. Richardson. Mr. Hickman presided at the organ. The Rev. T. Bishop spoke on "Possi- bilities of the Infinite." He alluded to the marvel- lous discoveries of the past century, showing that the impossibilities of byegone days were among the most ordinary things of to-day. We were, however, only using in fresh combinations the elements God gave us in the great storehouse of the world. So in the spiritual life, all things were possible with God. Touching upon the remarkable scenes witnessed in con- nection with the Welsh revival, he said that it would have been impossible for mere orators,poets and artists to hold the attention of congregations for such an ex- traordinary length of time, and with such good results. With God, however,all things were possible and thus it was:that the revival was having a great effect upon the daily life of the people.</p>
        <p>Selections from " Elijah?' A snecial feature of the festival was the rendering on Monday evening of selections from Mendelssohn's grand oratorio" Elijah." There was a good attendance. Mr. R. A. Naylor presiding. The meniners of the choir, under Mr. Frank Hickinan's conductorship, again acquitted themselves with marked credit, and the choruses were all adequately interpreted. - Particularly fine was the stirring Baal chorus" and the vocalists thoroughly entered into the spirit of the work. The performance throughout gave evidence of careful train- ing and painstaking preparation, and was a fitting conclusion to the services of the festival. The soloists are deserving of a special measure of praise. Mrs. Arthur Booth's sweet soprano voice found full scope in" Hear ye Israel" which was expressively treated. Miss Mabel Henshall (contralto) was responsible for a feeling, interpretation of the beauti- ful solo "0 rest ia. the cord" and she was also heard to advantage in" Woe, woe unto them." Miss Carrie Hastings, who has already become well-known as a soprano of much promise, pleasingly sang the short solo part "There is nothing. Mr. 'Greenough and Mr. Arthur Griffiths (tenors) did justice to the items " Ye people, rend your hearts " and "0 man of God respectively." The vocalisation of several trying parts was entrusted to Ur. E. H. Pearce,A.L.C.M. (bass) whoseefforts certainly merited the warm praise bestowed upon them. Perhaps he acquitted himself best in" Lord God of Abraham " and ais not his word like a fire?" Mr. S. L. Coveney againgbly presided at the organ. During an interval the Chairman delivered a short address. He remarked that it was a very gratifying sight to see so many young ladies and gentlemen willing to give their services for the praise of God. He was pleased to say that the new Wesleyan hymn book fittingly began with the grand old hymn " Oh, for a thousand tongues to sing." Was there anything loftier they could apply their faculties to than praising the name of the Great Redeemer. It was a great pleasure at all times to find a number of people gathered together to listen to the praise and to show their appreciation of those who rendered it. In these days there were some people who considered it rather beneath them even to acknowledge the great Creator of the Universe,and there never was a time when Christian people were more in duty bound to stand up and to declare their belief in the faith that was delivered to the saints. They must ever remember that their prais es on earth were but a prelude to the glorious praises before the throne hereafter. The REV. THEODORE BISHOP announced that the collections of the festival, including a donation of £lO from the chairman, amounted to £54 is. 6d. Last year the sum raised was £25.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN having bean heartily thanked for presiding, the singing of the Doxology concluded the service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CONTENTED DUTCHMAN.</head>
        <p>A CONTENTED DUTCHMAN.</p>
        <p>Faith is an excellent quality of mind, if based on correct judgment ; otherwise it may lead to unfortunate results. Who has not heard of the fat Dutchman on the sinking ship who, on being urged to hasten into the lifeboat, then waiting alongside to receive him, calmly smoked his pipe and remarked that he was in no hurry, because his life was insured in one of the best offices in Amsterdam? Verily, some people don't know a good , thing when they see it, while others recognise it on sight and promptly reach out and seek to obtain it. Probably no more extreme examples of misplaced faith can be found than among the suffering sick. An ailing man or woman who\ is attended by some respect- able doctor will more often than not be perfectly assured that everything possible is being done to recover their lost health; and this, perhaps, despite the fact that weeks or even months elapse without any progress whatever towards recovery. Wiser in his generation is Mr. Andrew Poulter, of Ash Vale, Aldershot. Mr. Poulter, writing on June 10th, 1896, relates a common experience, but which is none the less interesting on that account. "There can be nothing more certain in this world," he say, "than that I owe my life to Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. Up to the autumn of 1889 I was a strong, healthy man. Then somethin_g went wrong with me. My energy was gone and I was easily exhausted. My appetite also left me, and the little food I ate gave ma pain across the chest and between the shoulders. I "As winter approached I was seized with rheumatism. Pains in my joints tormented me night and day,so that I got little or no sleep. I was attended by a doctor, who gave me medicines and recommended linseed-meaf poultices. When it was found that I was no better for this treatment, I was told to wait until the warm weather came. "During 1899 I got along somewhat better, but towards the close of 1891,1 had a second attack, more severe than the first. It made a helpless cripple of me. I was incapable of work, and so irritable from pain that I could not bear anyone to speak to me. My doctor told me plainly that I could not live much longer. Mr. C. Harwood, of Ash Vale, did not agree with this opinion, and brought me a bottle of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. After taking this medicine for a few weeks I could eat well. Gradually all my pains left me and my former good health returned." Eight years have done nothing to impair the perman- ency of Mr. Poulter's cure, for so recently as 25th April, 1904, he writes to say : "I continue to enjay sound bodily health, and am at present as well and strong as ever in my life." The wrecked Dutchman's faith was based upon mis- conception Mr. Harwood's recommendation upon a universally acknowledged truth.</p>
        <p>CHILD KNOCKED SENSELESS BY ITS FATHER. Thomas Lloyd, collier. Hope-street, was fined R 2 and costs at St. Helens. on Monday, for assaulting his little boy, two and a half years old. Mr. Smith prosecuted for the Children's Society, and said that while husband and wife were having .a trivial dispute the child cried, and the husband knocked him unconscious by a blow on the head.</p>
      </div>
