      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PORT ARTHUR'S PLIGHT.</head>
        <p>PORT ARTHUR'S PLIGHT.</p>
        <p>So often have we been promised the fall 01 t Arthur during the last few months that ,ve had begun to look upon it as bearing a ±armed life, and •as a consequence the result )f the capture of 203-Metre Hill' was not :ully walised by non- military -people. But t has since been proved that it was tanta- mount to the fall of th fortress, insomuch as t is now really immaterial how long it holds 203-Metre Hill is the position which</p>
        <p>:ornmands the harbour, and as scx3n as the</p>
        <p>japanese got their heavy guns mounted it )ecame obvious that the harbour was unten- t, ible. The bombardment at the end of last week not only rendered the Russian fleet ineffective, but totally destroyed it. The ." Bayan " was set on fire and half capcized. The " Retvisan " and the " Poltava ", were submerged at high tide. up to the conning tower. The " Pallada " and " Pobieda " heeled over to port and starboard respectively awing to their injuries, and at high tide their Jecks are washed. The " Peresviet " also is artially submerged at high tide, while the Gilyak " is aground close to the shore and listing over considerably. The " Sevastopol " was removed to anchorage in the outer road- stead to avoid the bombardment, but she, too, is ineffective. The result of this is that the Russians now have no naval base free from ice, nor have they any effective fleet to join the Baltic Fleet upon its arrival, should it ever arrive, while .on the other hand Admiral Togo is free to leave Port Arthur, except for a few torpedoes to prevent blockade-running, and can sail out to meet the enemy wherever it uits him best. The consequence is twofold. Firstly, it is rumoured that the Baltic Fleet will be re- called, or at any rate ordered to dawdle and not to arrive ; while, secondly. the. .Japanese will make no further assaults upon Port Ai-1111r, lint will starve out the garrison, which has had to re:reat to the Liaotishan nitittla. It is a terrible position. Port Ar hur is a nia,ss (.1 pu'tifving corpses and hill of wc;iiii(',cd, whiL• food, appears to be -carce and v. ater evet,. scarce r, but Liaotishan iiregnid, and the japatiese do -not see he u--c of :-acriticing more lives after their (T.orn-:eus loses. Again, the big guns of the riu,! i:uis have been o quiet. of late that it !'llue,ls a !--lic•tine-ls of ammunition.. The</p>
        <p>J r.e,e 11ve to face rile :-evere loss in the ur.enf ef 1,61- triumt It, as the cruiser</p>
        <p>" S-2.iven " z± mechanical mine and was tkwil t 7-0 wi hsc ricus 1n- f lifp. This males 1a iifi nr et,,•d (i r Iv! eighth, including ran- poi is) wh;ch he .lai-aneGe have lost</p>
        <p>. . ince the beginning c f the war, but, taken all round, it is a small price to pay for the tot al desiruction of the 1-,!nt;s-ian fleet. It is -aid that hould '•: tie fleet have the temerity ti' go on, 2, • . • Togo has a new ort of torpo.:o them at the Petcadores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>H.M.S. BRITANNIA.</head>
        <p>H.M.S. BRITANNIA.</p>
        <p>In these days of lie,m interest in all .nava matters the launch .of a first - class battle- ship is quite an event. On Saturday the Marchioness of Londonderry, accompanied by Lord Selborne. and other leading officials. launched the very latest. H.M.S. " Britannia," from Portsmouth Dockyard, christening it with a bottle of colonial wine. When corn pleted. the " Britannia " will be one of the fi 'lest battleships in the whole world. She is a ifister to the " King Edveird V 11.," and is of the nominal displacement of 16,500 tons without her coal. Her leng:h is 425 feet, and her breadth 78 feet, and her engines will be of t 6,500 horse-power,. giving .her a speed of eighteen knots an , hour. Her sixteen boilers will •be fitted with water tubes, and oil fuel will be carried as well as Iwo thousand tons of l, coal. Her armament will be enormous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NEW BATTLESHIP</head>
        <p>THE NEW BATTLESHIP</p>
        <p>She is to carry four twelve-inch guns, each capable of throwing an 850-pound shell through 42 inches of wrought iron or thirteen inches of steel. She will also have four 9.2 guns, capable of a 380-pound shell of an almost equally great power of penetration. There will also be ten seven-inch and ten six-inch • guns in addition to smaller ones, and the " Britannia " in action, broadside on, will be able to pour into her opponents over five tons of burst shells a minute! She is armour pia:rod all over with a depth of from seven to nine inches- of the finest Krupp steel. A feature is to be the absence of fi gh t r, g tojy. bu t her cost will be enormous. The " Britannia " will be worth a million and a half by the time she is finished, or half as much again as the present flagships of our leadinc, fleets The launching went Oft without a hitch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>LAST OF THE POLLARD CASE.</head>
        <p>THE LAST OF THE POLLARD CASE.</p>
        <p>At length the end of the Pollard 'case has b -en reached by the dedision of the Attorney- General to enter a 'nolle prosequi " in the case of Osborn, the solicitor, about whom the jury disagreed at the October trial. He was to have been put upon his trial again this week by himself at the Central Criminal Court, but on Saturday the Attorney- General's de- cision not to proceed was an- nounced. He was .chnrged with conspiring to pervert the course of justice and wrongfully and unjustly to procure a decree for the dissolu- tion of the nmr-</p>
        <p>riage ofi Kate Pollard and Thomas Pollard, and after the money which has been spent on the case Put of the public funds- and the comments of :he judge, it is, to say the least of it, a matter of grave surprise to the public that the opinion of a second jury was not taken apon a case of such national 'importance. The case has gone on now for many months, apart from the original divorce suit, and the public has followed it with the keenest</p>
        <p>iikaA</p>
        <p>.7..„ i , -•"^ , . 1\ "irt..4iitta.:\ se SBORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
