      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 17, 1664.</p>
        <p>neatly to Wattie Davies, who romped :round the oppos- ing backs, awl scored near the posts. Goodall added the extra paints. At the interval Batley were leading by 5 points to Halifax nil. With the altered conditions in their favour, Halifax \tvere expected to do better, but the passing of their backs left much to be desired. Result :—Batley 1 goal andl tr3(s points), Halifax nil. OLDHAM V. BROUGHTON RANGERS AN EXCITUG GAME WON BY THE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Oldham, before 5,000 spectators.--'ih-e-gin-ae throughout was keenly fought, and provided an excit- ing finish. Prater kicked off, and Oldham at once attacked. Nothing came of this effort,and the Rangers made a brief incursion into. the home half. From here they were repulsed, and then 0/dham,_playing strongly, worked back intothe visitors' "25." Here Arthur Lees neatly made a mark. and Ferguson opened the scoring by kicking a goal. The ball hit the upright, but re- bounded inside. Superior back play by the Rangers then led to two tries in rapid succession by Harris and Horton, from the firit of which Harris kicked a goal. Notwithstanding this reverse, Oldham played up. strongly and had more of the game than their opporr- eats. Ferguson kicked a penalty goal before half-tune, and at the interval the Rangers led by 4. points. The second half saw Oldham often on tbe attack, but unable to break through the excellent defence of the. visitors. Ferguson kicked a third goal, and exciting play followed towards the finish of the game; which ended with the score:—Broughton Rangers 1 goal and 2 tries (3 points), Oldham 3 goals (6 points):</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WIGAN v, RUNCORN,</head>
        <p>WIGAN v, RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>Played at Central Park,where 8,000 per—sons assembled. The Wigan team included Bert Jenkins, who has been recruited from the Mountain Ash district of -,outh Wales. Runcorn had all the best of the game in the initial half. Early on Houghton tried to lower the I Wigan goal, and immediately after Butterworth struck the upright. Then a fine run by Jones placed the home line in jeopardy, whilst Moran got clear, but ran the leather dead. Mason cleared time after time, but .Tones ought to have scored, as he had the line at his mercy. Eventually Wigan made a rush to the other end, and 1 one of the few passing bouts indulged in ended in Vickers registering a try. Wigan were subsequently penalised, and Butterworth landed a goal, and a similar penalty falling to Wigan Mason lowered the Runcorn' citadel. However Moran succeeded in crossing the home line, and Butterworth kicking another goal, the Cestrians led at the interval by 7 points to 5. There was a decided change in the second half, as Wigan played a bustling game, with the result that the visitors seldom became dangerous. Time after time the Runcorn line •was in danger, and ultimately Mason landed a goal, which was followed by a try secured by Lowe. Result—Wigan 2 goals and j. tries (10 points), Runcorn 2 goals and 1 try (7 points). Teams : Wigan—Mason, back; Lowe' Windsor Jones, Jenkins, and Leytharn, three-quarters ; Hopkins and ileave, half backs • Hilton, Barton, Collier, Whittaker, Topping, Tyrer;Vickers and Blears, forwards. Runcorn—Houghton, back ; Moran, Hough, Butter- worth and Jones, three-quarter backs; Watton and Heiketh, half backs: Walker, Moulton, Richardson; Tomlinson, Dixon, Higgins, Dobson, and Kennedy, forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. Gibson, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. Taylor, Leigh, and Little, St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" V. SA.LF3RD "A."</p>
        <p>Played at Warrington on Saturday. Salford started, and the Warringtomans immediately took up the attack. A round of passing among the home backs ended in. Jordan being brought down within a yard of the line. From the ensuing scrummage Berry passed to Short, who scored behind the posts. Knight failed at goal. Another round of passing ended in Jackson scoring at the corner. Knight landed a goal from a difficult angle. Warrington kept up the pressure, and Berry added to the score by crossing at the corner. The home forwards dribbled away, and Harmer got over, but the point was disallowed. Bab Bate bluffed the opposition and scored Knight landing a goal. Short dribbled away, and after beating all ,opposition kicked into touch. Bate scored again, and Knight lauded another goal. Half-time: Warrington "A," 21 points; Salford "A," nothing. On resuming, Warrington pressed, and a good movement between Bate, Berry and Short ended in the latter scor- ing behind the posts, but Knight failed at goal. War- rington kept up the pressure, and Jackson was brought down twice in quick succession , within a few yards of the line. A fine passing mac ement between Berry, Bate, and Short saw the last-named pushed into touch at the corner. In the last quarter, Warrington had matters all their own way. Lunt and Berry scored tries, and just before the finish Jackson added two more. Result : Warrington "A," three goals, ten tries, 36 points ; Salford "A," nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>N.U. INTERNATIONAL MATCH.</head>
        <p>THE N.U. INTERNATIONAL MATCH.</p>
        <p>Warrington Men to the Fore.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday a meeting of the Northern League Com- mittee was held at Huddersfield, when the following teams for the international match at Bradford on January 2nd, 1905. were chosen England: IL W. Poole (Broughton Rangers), back; F. Spottiswoode (Oldham). R. Wilson (Broughton Rangers), J. Mosby (Bradford), and J. Fish (Warring- ton), threequarter backs; G. H. Marsden (Bradford), and J. Lomas (Salford), half backs; A. Starks (Hull Kingston Rovers), J. Ferguson (Oldham), S. Walker (Runcorn), G. H. Langhorn (Halifax), W.Morton (Hali- fax), H. Feather (Bradford), A. S. Boardman (Warring- ton), and Brook (Hunslet), forwards. Reserved: W. Little (lialifax),back ; J. Butterworth (Runcorn),centre threequarter back; J. Leytham (Wigan), wing three- quarter back; T. liockenhall (Warrington), half back; J. Preston (Warrington), and Moulton (Runcorn), forwards.</p>
        <p>Other Nationalities: G. Gunn (Wales and Bradford), back; A. E. Freer '(lrish International and Hull), P. Thomas (Wales and Leeds).T. D. Llewellyn (Wales and Leeds), and J. Dechan (Wales and Bradford), three- quarter backs; Hopkins (Wales and Wigan), and S. James (Wales and Broughton Rangers), half-backs; A. Laidlaw (Scotland and Bradford), H. P. Osborne (Wales and Hull Kingston Rovers), Dal Thomas ( \Vales and Oldham), Dal Rees (Wales and Salford), G. Thomas (Wales and Warrington), A. Scott (Scotland and Broughton Rangers), and H. Buckler (Wales and Salford), fornrds. Reserves:_ R. L. Thomas (Wales and Oldham), full-back ; W. Jones (Wales and Wigan), centre three-quarter back; Wharton Davies (Wales and Batley), wing; R. Burchall (Wales and Hull). half-back; F. H. f!Shugars (Wales and Warrington), and G. Prater (Scotland and Oldham). Officials : Referee, Mr. W. M'Cutcheon (Oldham) ; touch judges, J. Little (Halifax) and J. H. Houghton (St. Helens).</p>
        <p>GENEROUS GRANTS TO THE WIDOWS OF RICHARDSON AND BOARDMAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night a meeting of the general committee of the Northern Union was held at Huddersfield, under the presidency of Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes). An ap- plication for financial assistance on.behalf of the widow and children of the late George Boardman, Leigh, was made by the Leigh Club. A grant of 25 guineas was made. A sum of 25 guineas was also granted to the widow and children of the late J. Richardson, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. Benedict's Church.</p>
        <p>Special services were held at St. Benedict's Church, on Sunday when a new high altar was opened. Special sermons were preached by the Rev. Father Colman Mac Sweeney, O.F3L, who made an earnest appeal for the funds of the mission. The altar had been tastefully decorated by members of the congregation with an abundant supply of flowers. Special hymns were sung by an augmented choir from the various churches under the conductorship of Mr. Gibbins. Mr. Joseph Richardson presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>Rev. John Yonge and the Welsh Revival.' Preaching at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening. the Rev. John Yonge specially appealed to young people to let religion dominate their lives. He observed that the tidings which came to them in the columns even of the secular Press, of what God was doing anion..' the people of Wales, should arouse not only interest, but anxious enquiry. All through \Vales the young people were , flocking into the Kingdom of Christ. They were crowding the churches day by day, night by night; singing, praying, testifying, surrendering to the Saviour. They were foregoing the quest of the popular amusements. Football teams were breaking up, and matches were abandoned. Billiard tables, publichouses, were being deserted. Wherever one went the voice of hymn and prayer was heard, not only in the churches but in the coalmines, out in the streets, in the houses, everywhere. People were making up their quarrels, paying their debts, turning from drunkenness to soberness,from profanity of language to purity of speech. Such scenes had not been witnessed in the remembrance of living men. And the young people were in the van, leading the way. They had caught the Spirit of God, and were surrendering to Him in scores and hundreds. WhyZshould</p>
      </div>
