      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>•? BUR.</head>
        <p>•? BUR.</p>
        <p>interest. First came the King's Proctor's sensational intervention, and a most extra- ordinary light was thrown upon the methods of Slater's Detective Agency. After that Scott (alias Slater), Osborn, and some of the detectives were arrested on the above charge. All through the summer the case dragged on at the 'police court till the prisoners were committed for trial at the October sessions at the Old Bailey. Scott (alias Slater) was acquitted after some severe strictures from the judge, but his employes were sentenced to various terms of imprison- ment. Over Osborn the jury disagreed, and v::,w the Attorney-General has entered a " none prosequi." The chief thing to be said in its favour is that it brings to an end one of the most unsavoury cases of modern times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NAVAL REFORM.</head>
        <p>NAVAL REFORM.</p>
        <p>It was felt when Sir John Fisher toox up the post of First Sea Lord at the Admiralty that considerable changes might be expected, and now they have come even sooner than was anticipated. On Saturday the Earl of Selborne, the First Lord of the Admiralty, issued a highly , important Parliamentary gaper dealing with the mobilisation and dis- tribution of the Fleet. This question' of distribution, upon which is dependent the other of mobilisation, has practically not been altered since the days of sailing ships, whereas steam has changed all the problems of war, and it is hoped by the system just propounded to obtain greater value at a smaller cost. The present Home Fleet is to be called the Channel Fleet and is to have its headquarters at home. If is to consist of e oattlesnips, ana to it will be affiliated a squadron of six cruisers. The pr esent Channel Fleet is to be called the Atlantic Fleet and will be based at Gibraltar. It will consist of eight battleships with an affiliated</p>
        <p>\</p>
        <p>....:: _ ,-- ~..... ~....- A,,,,. ~.. ~...„ ...,,,,,_,,,-,,, ... „...__. .:....7, \ A ..,„_, --::, -i ...000.01,./t\ \., lIMI '4.4 ‘' /\\ :i iii 14 If</p>
        <p>A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LORD SELBORNE.</head>
        <p>LORD SELBORNE.</p>
        <p>squadron of six cruisers. The Mediterranean Fleet will have its base at Malta and consist of eight battleships with a squadron of large cruisers attached to it. The details of the China Fleet are to be announced later on, and the cruisers on the Australian, East Indian, Cape, North American, and West Indian stations will all be rearranged. Only first-class ships of all types will be put into commission, and the period of commission will be reduced to two years. Lord Sel- borne declares that the result will be an immediate access of efficiency to the Navy, which will be ready for war at a moment's notice, while at the same time he hopes to present to Parliament greatly lessened esti- mates next year. There is a strong rumour in naval circles that the Prince of Wales will hoist his flag in command of the Cruiser Squadron, with Admiral Poe as his second in command early next year, but nothincg is confirmed yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SYVETON ,TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE SYVETON ,TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>• Less than a month ago an extraordinary scene occurred in ,the French Chamber, one of the deputies, a M. Gabriel Syveton, strik- ing General Andre, who was then Minister or War. In consequence the Minister resigned, and M. Syveton was suspended and put on his trial.</p>
        <p>The trial was fixed for last</p>
        <p>Friday, but on</p>
        <p>Tliuri'day after-</p>
        <p>noon_t the</p>
        <p>was found dead</p>
        <p>in his study</p>
        <p>asphyxiated by</p>
        <p>funs from the gas-stove. H e had been very busy all the morning, and</p>
        <p>hadseenall</p>
        <p>sorts of people • and in addition.</p>
        <p>he had many appoint men ts</p>
        <p>for tlp after-</p>
        <p>noon, sc; at lunch he announced</p>
        <p>intention of lying down for an hour's rest. When his wife called him she found him dead on the floor. The gas-pipe was shown to be defective, but, strangely enough, the ventilation line was found to be stuffed up with newspapers of various dates, in- cluding that very day's ! Immediately the alarm was given, and police, magistrates, and doctors arrived. Three . theories were immediately advanced : 'accidental death, nicide, and murder. The more violent of the Nationalist, papers openly adopt, the last theory, And accuse the. Freemasons of the crime, who, they say, were afraid of the revelations M. Syveton would have made at his.trial had it, taken place. On the other hand, it is . known that within the last fortnight he insured his life heavily, but, he was in the best of spirits and confident of acquit- tal. His friends all scout any .idea of suicide, and M. Boncard, the examining magistrate, favours the idea that the whole thing VMS an accident. The verdict at the inquest was an open one, simply giving the cause of death as asphyxiation by gas. So fai the whole is shrouded in 'mystery, and is one of the greatest political sensations France has ever known. M. Syveton Was buried on Saturday with military honours in the presence of a large concourse of people. The dead deputy was only forty years of age and was a Pxofessoi of the Rheims College, but Lost his position owing to ,his violent attitude against the Dreyfus Revision. M. Guyot de Villeneuve, recognising his ability, continued his salary out of his own pocket so as to permit him to go into politics, where almost at once he made his mark.</p>
        <p>\s, k - • • • _ SYVETON..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION,</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION,</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE. First Divisiol.</p>
        <p>r SALFORD v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Salford met for the first time this season at Weaste. The weather was cold and threaten- ing, and there was only a moderate gate of about 4,000. Warrington were without Dickenson, for whom Brookes came in at right centre, while the home team turned out as selected. The ground was decidedly soft and rather against an open game. Teams and officials Salford: Lambert, back; Lomas, Thomas, Richards, and Baker, three-quarter backs; Preston and Haddocks, half backs; Tunney, Rhapps, Lewis, H. Buckler, A. Buckler, Brown, Rees, and Foster, forwar4s. Warrington: Hallam, back • Harris, Brooks, Isher- wood, and Fish, three-quarter backs ; Hockenhall and "Di " Davies, half backs; Belton, Shugars, Cook, Thomas, Naylor, Jolley, Preston and Boardman, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes).—Touch judges, Messrs. J. H. Houghton (St. Helens) and J. H. Bruck- shaw (Stockport). Lomas commenced operations with a lofty kick, and Davies' return was charged dOwn, but Brooks cleared smartly. Warrington soon gained the upper hand, Fish finding touch near the home line, but after some loose play Salford rushed the ball to midfield. Hockenhall and Davies opened out the game, but Isherwood and Fish both dropped passes through the coil and wet. The Warrington forwards put in an effective dribble which was only checked in time by Lambert, the Salford full back. The hApTe side was penalised in front of goal, but Preston railed with an easy shot. Warrington still continued to do the major portion of attacking, Fish and Harris both being tackled close in after smart pass- ing. Just before the interval, Salford reached the Warrington quarter and were with difficulty kept at bay. Half-time no score. bavies restarted, and the homesters with the aid of the breeze soon assumed the offensive. Warrington defended resolutely, however, Hallam checking a couple of forward rushes in masterly fashion. Play was gener- ally in Salford's favour, and Preston did excellent work at half back,but passing was at a discount.Lomas tried a drop at goal, but missed the object and Elarris touched down. Gradually, Warrington worked their way to the Salford end where several determined efforts were made to cross, but the hand to hand work was too slow to beat the defence. Salford came away finally with a rush, and Preston, Thomas and Lomas handling smartly, the Warrington defence was pierced. Lomas easily eluded Fish and got over near the goal after a remarkable corkscrew run. The scorer added a goal,and five minutes later the game ended with Salford winning by 5 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SWINTON v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>The Leeds team visited the Swint.an ground and after having the best of the forward play against the wind, collapsed ingloriously, and were easily beaten. There were about 3,000 persons at the match. Swinton having won the toss, Mosley started the game against the wind. The Leeds forwards putting a lot of energy and dash into their work, did most of the attacking for the first half-hour. A penalty kick was awarded,and Evans landed a goal, the ball striking the far post and rebounding over the cross-bar. This incident enabled Swinton to lead at half-time by a goal to nothing. Playing well together the Swinton forwards gradually wore their opponents down. After a short round of passing, Gartrell, on the right wing, eluded his opponents and scored a good try near touch. From this difficult position, and against the wind, J. Evans landed a goal. Shortly afterwards W. Evans made the mistake of attempting to kick out from his own goal with two opponents in front of him. The kick was charged, and Wallwork was presented with a very simple try, from which J. Evans failed to score a goal. Near the close a scramble in the Swinton quarters produced a try to Watts. Little- wood failed to add the goal points, and the end came with the score—Swinton 2 goals, 2 tries (10 points), Leeds 1 try (3 points.) ST. HELENS V. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>1 A fast and exciting game between these t-li-e-s-e- teams was mitnessed by a fair attendance at St. Helens. Playing with the wind the Lancashire men at once set up a strong attack, and while they were thus engaged Mosby and Eagers started some defensive passing, the ball I being finally thrown to Dechan on the Bradford quarter I line. With a fine strong run the Bradford wing man I dashed through the opposition,and after travelling three- parts of the length of the ground he scored behind the Iposts, for Eagers to kick a goal. St. Helens returned to the attack immediately on the re-start, and after narrowly missing a score on several occasions I a smart round of passing begun by Briers I ended in Banks scoring. The goal kick was not difficult, but Hall missed it. However, a little later Hall got the ball from the rear of the scrummage, and dropped a very smart goal. This put the teams on an equality of points, and though St. Helens pressed hotly thescores were still level at the interval. Bradford's superior weight and 'greater resource behind the scrum- mage began to tell atale in the second half, and after a long period of pressure they scored three tries in almost as many minutes. Two of the tries were , improved, one by Mosby and the other by Eagers. I Though the fight was now quite hopeless for them St. I Helens attacked again vigorously, Bradford being short 'of Turner injured, and Traynor scored a try after a ' long run by Hunter. This made the final score:- 1 Bradford 3 goals and 4 tries (18 points), St. Helens 1 i goal and 2 tries (8 points). i HIINSLET v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Hunslet. The spectators only numbered about 4,000. The opening play was of a very scrambling order, but by dint of some effective dribbling Hunslet at length established themselves in front of the Leigh posts. Hunslet commenced a long attack, which cul- minated in a try to Ward. Walter Goldthorpe suc- ceeded with the shot at goal. On the re-start effective passing was witnessed between Aloert Goldthorpe, Everson, and Ward. Leigh, however, tackled keenly, and it was not until another scramble took place at the visitors' end that Hunslet added a second score. This came about through Everson picking up smartly in the loose. He passed to Albert Goldthorpe, who, in turn, transferred to Walsh, and the last-named went over the line with a rush. Walter Goldthorpe again kicked the goal, giving Hunslet a lead of 10 points, which they retained.</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY: NARROW WIN FOR THE HONIE TEAM.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 1,000 spectators. Just before the interval Widnes pressed, and S. Aspey just failed to drop on the ball when over the line, half-time arriving with the score sheet blank. The second half i opened n favour of Widnes. The backs passed well, and t 4ice in quick snacession Wailing was pushed in touch when near the line. A touch kick put Wakefield on the offensive, until Taylor running the length of the field with the ball at hi g teet, was robbed by Metcalfe on the line. Subsequently Wakefield were penalised, and Tilley kicked a goal from a difficult position. Towards the close Widnes had the best of the game,and it was only by brilliant defensive work that they .were kept from scoring. Result :—Widnes 1 goal (2 points), Wakefield nil. tl4 BATLEY v. HALIFAX: SMART VICTORY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BY BATLEY.</head>
        <p>BY BATLEY.</p>
        <p>• At one time an encounter between Batley and Halifax would have drawn as 15ig a gate as any interclub con- test in Yorkshire. Thit interest in their doings has sadly diminished was, however, evident from the small gate which patronised :%loant Pleasant on Saturday. The attendance was actually the smallest of the season. 04k1and picked up behini the crummage, and threw - t</p>
