      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>it not be so h&amp;c ? In the Master's name he called the young people to whom his sermon was particularly addressed to conversion and decision now.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the above was held on Sunday last, when there was a fair attendance. Mr. T. Birchall, J.P.. presided, and said he hardly expected, 12 months ago, when he presided over theirmeeting, that he would be able to again preside. However, he was glad to be with them again, and to show his sympathy with tha P.S.A. work. He said he had thought much of late, in his quiet moments, how serious it was to leave undone so much that ought to be done. The evils within and around the masses of working people, as well as others, needed-willing hands and loving hearts, as well as self- sacrificing lives, to put them on to a higher plane.—The Rev. C. Harvey Cook was the speaker,and said that as he now stood in the pulpit of a Congregational Church, he might tell them, what some of them already knew, that he had been twelve years a minister of that denomination —twelve very happy years. He then spoke upon the "New Charity" which many were preaching at the present time, which meant "Don't let us talk of our differences. Let us talk of our agreements." He did not believe in that. Let them talk of their difbrenses but in the spirit of the Apostolic ethic "Speak the truth in love."He believed as a man,and spoke as a man, and claimed liberty and freedom.to think and speak for himself fea,rlessly yet lovingly, the truth as it presented itself to him.. What he claimed for himself, he would allow in others.—Mr. J. Ball and Rev. John Yonge also took part in the service. Mr. R. H. Pearson rendered a violin solo and Mrs. Pearson very pleasingly sang "The Star of. Bethlehem." The president gave the usual welcome to three new members, the total ' now being 566,f0ur more than last year.A collection was taken for the funds of the P.S.A. Considering the many attractions of the day, the gathering was very good:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>St. Oswald's Church, Winwick.</head>
        <p>St. Oswald's Church, Winwick.</p>
        <p>At, the usual morning service at the above church on Sunday, anew and beautiful oak lectern was dedicated. The Rector (Rev. Canon Penrhyn), preaching from, the test" Behold, I send my messenger before Thy face, which shall prepare thy way before Thee," explained that the lectern was in the memory of the late Mrs. Comber, of Myddleton Hall, and' her !husband, who rested in the adjoining church- yard. He had not the pleasure of knowing Mr. Comber, but in common with many of them he had known the late Mrs. omber well, and though latterly she was unable to occupy her usual place in the church, she never ceased to take the warmest interect in it,and in. the parish generally. Not in this parish only had her loss been felt, but her alms were widely distributed, and'in most of the poorer neighbouring parishes showas always ready to give her help to every good work, and close to the place where she was accustomed to join in their worshio,they had'a memorial of her. 2 Inland revenul... Mr. Browne, for fn. defence, submitted that, the Board of Inland' Revenue had decided by letter that the fact., 'as disclosed with reference of Purslow did not warrant any conviction.</p>
        <p>Mr. Simpson objected to the production of the letter, and said he did not know anything at all about it. The defendant said he relied on the letter of the Commissioners to the effect that he was not liable to take out a licence fo- Putslow, and said that with regard to both Billington and Vernon he only employed them as labourers partly on the farm and partly in his garden. The magistrates announced that they found Billington and Vernon were not male servants within the meaning of the Act, but that Parslow was a servant, and.was therefore, liable for an excise licence. The defendant would be fined 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Simpson applied for costs and suggested five guineas, as he had to come from London especially and bring witnesses from Northwich, Chester, Runcorn and Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mc. Browne opposed, and seed he had never heard sach an extraordmavy application, in his 25 years' experi- ence of Daresbury Petty Sessions.as that of an advocate specifying the costs himself. Inasmuch as he (Mr. Browne) had won on two points out of three he con- sidered he ought to have costs. The Bench conferred with the Clerk and decided to grant three guineas costs to the prosecution to cover the costs in this case and the case in regard to carriage licences.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne applied for a case to be stated for the Divisional Court or in appfnl tot the Quarter Sessions, and it was arranged with Mr. Greenall that such notices should be served upon him. Mr. Browne also intimated that the Automobile Club in London would take the matter up. as it was one of importance to motor owners all over the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NURSE IN PEACE AND WAR.</head>
        <p>THE NURSE IN PEACE AND WAR.</p>
        <p>Everyone believes a Nurse. I;"ar Correspondents praise her; those who need her help in Peace trust her implicitly. Hence the following words from a trusted and intelligent hospital helper. Nurse Ilrailsford, of Horsforth, near Leeds, are worthl of attention : "In the course of my long eKperience," said the Nurse, "I have met with many cases in which Dr. Williams' pink pills have proved boon to sufferers. I recall one case in which this medicine effected a re- mazkable cure. A young lady had suffered six months from an ulcerated stomach following antemia. She was as pale as a sheet, and looked as if she had not a drop of blood in her.</p>
        <p>"The invalid came to stay with me. he was as weak as a kitten, unable to walk many yards without assistance. I had to take her for drives at first to get her:into the fresh air. Her digestion was so poor she had to live on liquid food. Just before she came to me, her mother had bought her a box of Dr. William' pink pills for pale people, and she began to take them regularly. As she seemed entirely bloodless, she could not do better than take a medicinelwhich makes new blood. These pills are just what anatmic girls require. After a few days, she began to drink milk and eat solid food. Slowly but surely she regained her appetite. Her stomach became stronger, and, as it was able to retain solid food, sh,e soon gained energy, and was able to walk some distance. The paleness of her face was gradually replaced by a healthy colour. After three months with me, the young lady went home absolutely cured. When her mother saw her she exclaimed. never saw a girl so much improved in my life.' Dr. Williams' pink pills were the sole means of her cure. I can vouch /or the fact that she took no other medicine." Whole families sometimes fall victims to antemia men are not exempt. Dr. Williams' pink pills supply rich blood to the starved veins and onicklv banish many of the weaknesses and pains that afflict the weaker sex. But they are equally valuable for men, having cured indigestion, anFemia, palpitations, bile, nervous depres- sion, sciatica, rheumatism, paralysis, St. Vitus' dance. and nervous disorders. Genuine pills bear the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. If pestered with substitutes, it is best to go to another shopkeeper; or, in case of doubt, send two shillings and ninepence for one box, or thirteen and ninepence for six boxes, to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Flolhorn- viaduct, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS TROPHY,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS TROPHY,</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Cairo-street Team.</p>
        <p>The cup presented by Mr. R. A. Naylor for competi- tion amongst the clubs connected with the Warrington Draughts League was on Monday elening. handed over to the Cairo-street team, who will hold it for twelve months. The cup, which is a very handsome one, bears the inscription :—" Presented to the Warrington and District Draughts League by R. A. Naylor, Esq." The Cairo-street Club are to be congratulated upon their success. At the presentation ceremony Mr. F. Dunne presided over a large attendance. • The CHAIRMAN, in the course of a brief speech, remarked that as an intellectual recreation, the game of draughts had no superior, and was well worthy of the countenance and support of those who had the best interests of the working classes, and especially the working youths, at heart. The game had no equal, save the sister game of chess. Both pastimes were eminently adapted for strengthening the reflective faculties and developing habits of caution and foresight in youth and I early manhood. Both games were free from the vice of gambling, and that alone would he a recommendation to the thinkitr, public. (Applause). He took that oppor- tunity of testifying their obligation to their president, Mr. Naylor, who had been their good friend ever since the formation of the League some eight years ago, and to whom they were indebted for the beautiful trophy- -a trophy that any club might well be proud to have the honour of winning and holding as an emblem of victory. (Hear, hear.) Mr. NAYLOR, who was received with applause, said he was very much interested in the doings of the League. I He had noticed that at the present time there was a I great discussion in the public press about games and pastimes. Some were strictly religious, and people of the present day were beginning to condemn anything in the way of recreation. But playing a game of draughts was a very innocent way of enjoyment after a hard day's work. It brought out their mental faculties, and he hoped to see the time when people would engage in the different kinds of games that would develop physical force and muscle, which were necessary for the pre- servatiOn of good'health. They would have a healthy mind if they had a healthy body, and the more they could do in that direction the better citizens they would become, and probably the better Christians. (Applause.) He wished the Draughts League every success, and hoped that men who were spending their time in a worse manner would become associated with it. (Applause.) He then handed the cup over to Mr. Plinston, the secretary of the winning team. Mr. PLINSTOX briefly replied, thanking Mr. Naylor for the splendid gift and also for attending at great inconvenience.</p>
        <p>Mr. lIDe-NNE proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Presidelq. . Mr. PLINSTON seconded and the vote was heartily b.ccorded.</p>
        <p>A sides match was afterwards played, the players being chosen by l‘fessrs. Dunne and Plinston.</p>
      </div>
