      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SKIN AND BLOOD PURIFICATION</head>
        <p>SKIN AND BLOOD PURIFICATION</p>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAV.—Before Colonel Ridgway (presiding) and Messrs. Henry Neild and Roger C. Parr. GAIIR TRESPASS.—WiIIiam Willett, veterinary surgeon, Stretton, was summoned for trespassing in search of game at Whitley, on land in the occupation* of Mr. Henry Neild on November 25th.—He pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Draper, solicitor, prosecuted, and stated that on the date in question the defendant's dog was seen hunting in the Ashfield wood by - one of the men,and immediately after the defendant came crushing through the bushes. The prosecutor had suffered con- siderably from this kind of thing and though he did not wish to press the case it should be made clear that the trespassing must be put a stop to.—Defendant in- dignantly denied the offence. He was not and never had been a poacher.—The magistrates decided to order defendant to pay the costs. A DANGEROUS PRACTICE. —Laura Fern, 40, Walton- road, Stockton Heath, was summoned for selling fire- works to a child under thirteen years of age.—Defend- ant who stated that the fireworks were slid by her son was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Socur..—A social was held on Saturday evening in connection with the Men's Bible Class of the Penketh Wesleyan Church, when a varied and interesting pro- gramme was provided. The president,Mr. C. E. Parker occupied the chair and spoke of the progress of the class and the individual interest which was displayed by the members. A pianoforte duet by Mr. George and Miss Weaver was much enjoyed, and a pianoforte solo by Miss Johnson (Sankey) was excellently given. Songs were rendered by Mr. D. Dunn and Mr. H. Plimpton, and Miss Emily Dunn sang " Daddy " with great effect. Recitations were given by Mr. G. Whitfield, of Stockton Heath, and Miss Annie Furness. of Warrington. Master W. S. Furness con- tributed a violin solo, which was well received, and two violin solos by Master Tom Barton were much appre- ciated. Mr. George Weaver, Master W. Pearce, and Miss Johnson were the accompanists. During the interval refreshments were served. At the conclusion of the programme, a vote of thanks, proposed by the Rev. John Gunnell and seconded by Mr. Birch. to the artistes and to all who carried out the arrangements, was carried unanimously. The committee were : Messrs. Walter, A. Seed, R. Long, Ashton, Dunabin, Wane, J. Wood, Holcroft, and Hobson.</p>
