        <p>TOWLE'S 0 HLOKODYNE RENOWNED FOR S eitt LORODY NE COUGHS, TOWLE' l. J INr LUENZA, TOWLE'S e lILORODY NE BRONCHITIS, CONSUMPTION, TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE ASTHMA, SPASMS, &amp;C. TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE 130. per Bane.</p>
        <p>question will not be raised in any form in the pre- sent Parliamint." No doubt; but to postpone the appeal to the country on this very issue as long as possible is what straightforward people call pro- crastination. It is good tactics, of course. It has enabled Mr. BALFOUR to use the persuasion of the blessed formula with some effect on the minds of Free Fooders. They are not all strong enough either to stand alone, or to join the Opposition in defence of a common interest. So when they are assured that Mr. BALFOUR is not a Protectionist, and that he is really pained to find old friends sus- pecting him of such a heresy, some of them are disposed to hark back, and accept the blessed formula which saves them from the unpleasant process of cutting themselves off from their party. It does not strike them as suspicious that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has also repudiated Protection. Like Mr. BALFOUR, he is a Free Trader who thinks that Free Trade can be harmonised with protective duties, provided they are called by some other name. There is an effort in some quarters to believe that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is sorely dis- appointed by the Edinburgh speech. We can see no evidence of that. Why should he be dis- appointed with a. blessed formula which, if it does not go the wholellength of preferential trade,would commit the country to a policy of shifting tariffs ?</p>
        <p>The Liberals of the district will be delighted to learn that Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., has promised to address a mass meeting in Warrington on February 3rd next. For some time, the local Liberal Association, assisted by Mr. A.H. Crosfield, have been endeavouring to secure Mr. Lloyd George,for a Parr Hall meeting, and their success will be hailed with gratification. As the most brilliant debater in the House of Commons, and a champion of freedom and progress, the member for Carnarvon Boroughs possesses a reputation throughout the Kingdom which is second to none. His visit on February 3rd should do much to rally the Liberal forces of Warrington for the coming battle.</p>
        <p>There have been some alarming rumours in the town this week with regard to educational matters and we hasten to assure our readers that the Board of Education has not descended upon the town with the wrath of Jove and swept away half the school grants. Nevertheless the actual position of affairs is sufficiently serious to cause real anxiety for the future. The accommodation difficulty has long been a bugbear, and it has been so frequently shelved that the Board of Education is fast losing patience. The town is dependent upon the Board for no less a sum than £22,000 in grants, and it will not do to trifle any longer with an authority which has power to with- draw all or any portion of this money. This year's grants are already overdue, and we understand that the Inspector has rec3mmended the Board not to pay the grant for Hamilton-street school, and to put the eight remaining schools which suffer from overcrowding on the minimum grant. This means a loss of £1,200 on the Hamilton street school and a shilling per scholar-2175—0n the other schools mentioned. The position of affairs in therefore sufficiently serious, though it falls, fortunately, a long way short of the cal- amitous state of things which was rumoured. A guarantee has to be given that the new schools in Orford and Latchford will be completed by August 1906, and it is also abundantly cle ir that no further shirking of the acconmodation question will be tolerated at headquarters. The Own is face to face with a grave responsibility, which can no longer be avoided. Economy in all depart- ments will require to be still more rigidly exercised and municipal enterprise more rigorously curtailed if the rates are to be kept within bounds.</p>
        <p>Friends and foes alike admit that Mr. Chamber- lain is a remarkable man, and he himself demon- strates the fact on every public appearance. There is no one, whom we are aware of, who can so coolly ignore a too-evident fact or twist the same nto the proverbial lie-and-a-half. On Thursday evening he delivered a speech of inordinate length to" a West-end audience in an East-end district," as Mr. John Burns pungently put it. The speech was the unmistakable grunt of the whole-hogger\ Protection was its keynote, but from beginning to end there was no mention of sugar. Well sugar is dearer, and sugar is one of the , first fruits of Mr. Chamberlain's policy of preference. By going up in price it has com- mitted a crime in the eyes of its self-appointed god-father, so poor sugar was ignored. The slight will probably hurt sugar less than the price of it hurts the poor people, many of whom greeted Mr. Chamberlain with hisses both on arriving and departing from the Limehouse meeting. They were not allowed in; Oh no! their place was with the sugar—outside. It can scarcely be believed that this champion of Protection, speak- ing in a poor district where every family feels the rise in sugar, uttered no word of apology for his first great toll of £8,000,000 from the p3ckets of the English people. But the truth is as indisput- able as on this occasion, it is ugly. Mr. Chamber- lain is truly a remarkable man.</p>
        <p>The resolution of the Property Owners Associa- tion was read at the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, and immediately stigma- tised by Mr. A. J. Jolley as "part of the disgracefill and discreditable agitation got up and engineered by methods which could only earn the scorn and contempt of every honest man." Mr. Jolley is making the serious and only too common mistake of imagining that the agitation against the Workhouse extension is the resuleof some personal enmity against himself. At least that is what we deduct from the tone of his remarks, and we are sure also that he is quite wrong. The opposition to the scheme in question is not even confined to the Property Owners' Association. A very large proportion of the rate- payers are of opinion that the time is not opportune for spen ling several thousand pounds on the Workhouse especiallyif, as is substantially asserted the required accommodation can be obtained without additional bricks and mortar. The Guar- dians inaview of public opinion and the unavoid- able expenditure on education, will do well to hesitate ere committing the ratepayers to an extra burden at this stage.</p>
        <p>Launched but a short time ago with a great flourish of trumpets, the Conservative Labour Party is doing very little in Warrington to justify ,the expectations of its promoters. It was evident from the smallness of the attend- ance and pronounced lack of enthusiasm at the public meeting on Wednesday, that the workingmen are not going to be led away by the Protectionist fallacies propounded by the spokesmen of this organization. Like the electors throughout the country they are far from being convinced that Protection is going to benefit British industry and improve the 13t of the work- ing-classes. They know only too well how infinitely better are the conditions of to-day as com- pared with those which obtained in this country under the old Protective regime and even as compared with the present state of labour in protected nations. It is under Free Trade that England's prosperity has been built up and the periodial Government returns are sufficient to destroy the illusion that our trade is, to use the of t- quoted expression," going to the dogs." Notwith- standing the campaign of the Conservative Labour Party which seeks to return to Parliament one who has declared himself an out and out Pro- tectionist, we are confident that Warrington will prove in no uumistakable way its adherence to Free Trade principles.</p>
        <p>The rapid growth of the use of the motor-car in this country has brought into especial prgrain- once a subject xhich has fo: long been a matter of considerable contention. The necessity of vehicles of all kinds to csrry lights t-) warn pedestrians or the occupants of other vehicles of their approach has been recognised for many years, and in most parts of the country by-laws cbtain whieh make the carrying of lights corn-</p>
        <p>pulsory. But the introduction of motor-cars has divided the vehicular traffic on the roads into two classes--the fast and slow. A fast vehicle coming up behind a slow one in the dark is—as was demonstrated very painfully in the recent accident to the Duke of Connaught—not unlikely to col- lide with it unless there be some special warning displayed. So long as the roads are used for mixed traffic this danger must exist, and it is not unreasonable, therefore on the part of those who travel in motor cars to ask that it shall be made compulsory for vehicles of all kinds to display a red light behind. The motor car has come to stay,and it will be well if the authorities recognise at this stage the necessity of making provision for the regulation of traffic to avoid accidents. Not less desirable than the measure which has already been spoken of is the provision that the law in regard to the carrying of lights should be made common to the whole of the country, and that it should be no longer left to loJal authorities to made what regulation they see fit. That was perhaps, well enough when long-distance and high-speed traffic was not seen on the country roads; but it is clearly out of date now. Motorists can fairly look for some assistance in this matter to the Home Office, to which they have applied by deputation.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes. At the local examination arranged and organised by the Incorporated Society of Musicians, held On the 28th of November last at the Old Academy, War- rington, Miss Agatha Richardson of 114, Butter- market-street presented four candidates for examina- tion in pianoforte playing, all of whom passed success- fully as follows :—Miss Pauline Caldwell prepara- tory; Miss Jennie Caldwell, grade I.; Miss Breatrice Cotton, grade IL; Miss Cecelia Caldwell, grade 11. —At the recent examination held in connection with the Victoria College of Music (Manchester), the follow- ing pupils of Miss Maud Jennings, Wilderspool Cause- way, passed successfully : Miss Norah Harding, 174, Battersby lane, (2nd division), Ist class with honours, 90 marks ; Miss Olivia Thompson, Alexandra Vaults, Scotland-road, (Ist division), Ist class with honours, 90 marks.</p>
        <p>YOU ARE SURE of getting good value in ALF TA.YLOR'S boots for everybody.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Brick-street Band of Hope Society.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above, the -3rd entertain- ment of the season was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. The chair was occupied by Mrs. Moores. The programme consisted of recitations given by Ethel Wainwright, Elizabeth Appleton, Robert Ellison and John Whittle. Two violin solos were rendered by Master S. Hibbert and selections on the phonograph were given by Master E. Smith. A song was given by Master Robert Ellison entitled "Joe Perkins." The entertainment concluded with two dialogues entitled "The drunkard's lament " and "Patrick v. Michael." XMAS PRESENTS.-Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street,Warrington.Splendid selection.—Advt. Warrington Women Liberals' Social.</p>
        <p>,The first of a series of social evenings arranged in connection with the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, was held at the Liberal Club on Monday evening. Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes presided, in the absence of Mrs. Armitage, over a good attend- ance. Miss Crosfield read a paper on "Educa- tion in the horn",' dealing principally with the education derived from the reading of good literature, and giving a list of books which she con• sidered were suitable for boys and girls in their teens. Mrs. Simpson read a paper on temperance, which caused considerable discussion, and Mrs. Holmes read a paper on "Cruelty to birds in connection with millinery." The social was a distinct success. The second of the series will be held on January 4th.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>HEATHER</p>
        <p>You CANNOT DO BETTER than buy your boots from ALP TAYLOR. He can satisfy you.—Advt. A Miner's Claim. An interesting case was heard at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, when William Johnson, 52, Clarence-street, Earlestown, sought to recover from the Collins Green Colliery Company 2s. 6d., damages for alleged unlawful detention in a mine.— Plaintiff's case was that on Septenber 2nd one of the haulage ropes in the mine broke at 8.30 a.m. and caused himself and about 50 other men to cease work. They went to the pit shaft but were refused per- mission to leave the mine, being detained until 3.20 p.m. The action was brought under the auspices of the Miners' Federation as a number of complaints had been received of colliery proprietors showing a want of consideration to their men in this respect. In cross-examination plaintiff admitted that he could have gone on " picking " coal though he could not have sent it to the pit shaft. —His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, held that in face of the fact that the plaintiff could have gone on. working after the accident he had no case, and gave judgment for the defendant with costs.—Plaintiff was represented by Mr. Kelly, Mr. Peace appearing for the defendants. PLENTY OF PEOPLE are wearing ALF TAYLOR'S BOOTS. Why not you ? 45, TSorsemarket street, Warrington.—Advt. Concert in St. James's Rho&amp;</p>
        <p>An excellent programme Was suoiuitted at a con- cert which was held in St. James' schoolroom, L atch ford, on Thursday evening,. .The entertainment was in aid of the Sunday school prize fund and attracted a fair attendance. Nfiss P. Houghton proved herself a finiShed vocalist and pianist, her vocal contributions ia particular being</p>
        <p>greatly . enjoyed. Mr. J. 1:..11:bilt added to his reputation as one of Warrington's most accomplished tenor singers, his efl-)rts being heartily encored. Mr. W. Simmond, a Proton sing+ of repute delighted the audience w I.li his ,Selectio:is, and the violin solos by Master Torn tlarton were also well received.. - Master. Bar 's juvenile orchestra also scored a distinct suocess. Miss B. Eckersley, who was principally responsible for the arranging of the concert, earner! the hearty applause of 'her auditors for her song,., as did also Miss L. Fairhurst, both proving themselves singers of no mean ability. Mr. E. A Sanders. was responsible for the humorous ele,Jient his eiTorts giving rise to considerable mirth. B. Wadsworth ably acted as accompanist. LADIES.' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGN-STREET.--Advt. Walker's Brewery Band Concert. - - •</p>
        <p>SHORTBREAD.</p>
        <p>Body Recovered from the River. The body of Frederiok Buchannon (19) of Lower Walton, was recovered from the river Mersey between Walton Arches and the works of the Arpley Chemical Company, yesterday morning. It will be remembered that Buchannon was dro,vneci about a month ago by falling from a dredger. He was the third member of the family drowned in the locality under almost identical circumstances. Ambulance Ball.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was filled almost to overflowing on Thursday night when the annual concert in connection with Messrs. WAlker's Brewery Band was held. The complete success which attended the function must have been exceedingly gratifying to all concerned, for the interesting programme met with the warmest approbation. It is pleasing t 3 note that the band under the able conductorship of Mr. P. J. Brady, continues to gain in efficiency, and the various selections were given with fine spirit. The auditor could not fail to be impressed with the careful rehearsal and good train- ing which the performances betokened. Special praise is due to Mr. J. Peterson for the cornet solo "The Alpine rose." Another organization which did itself marked credit was the brewery choir, in charge of Mr. T. Armstrong. Two glees were submitted with taste and expression and evoked hearty applause. The solo vocalists were Miss C. Hastings (soprano) and Mr. L. Aukland (bass), Two recalls were accorded Miss Hastings for sweet interpretations of" Sing on", (Denza) and "Rose softly blooming" (Sphor) and her encore items "The children's home " and " Home sweet home" were equally well received. Mr. Auk- land sang "Oh, oh, hear the wild wind blow" (Mattei) and" Son of mine "(Wallace) in meritorious style and he too was vigorously encored. He scored an even greater success in his rendering as an acknowledgment of the plaudits, of "The Wolf." Several banjo selections by Mr. Mangles proved a very popular feature, as was evidenced by the demand for an additional item. The humorist of the evening was Mr. A. Grounds, who soon established himself in the favour of the audience and created a great deal of mirth. His " quick-change " when representing a lady vocalist was exceptionally good The duties of accompanist were discharged by Mr Bert Smith.</p>
        <p>A ball in aid of the fWnds of the local corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade was held in the Gymnasium last evening. Dancing commenced at eight and was kept up until a late hour to music supplied by Mr. P. Randall's Band. The M.C.'s were Superintendents Baldry, and F. Taylor, and Sergeant Hanoway.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Ball. The second annual band ball in connection with the Ist V.B. (Prince of Wales Volunteers) South Lancashire Regiment, was held in the Drill Hall on Monday evening, when about 100 couples were present. Dancing to the strains of the Volunteer Band, under the direction of Mr. J. T. Richardson was kept up from 6 p.m to 2 a.m. Sergeant-Drummer 3. W- Plinston ably discharged the duties of MO. and the catering was in the hands of the ball cortimittee and gave every satisfaction.</p>
        <p>Loose and in Packets. Also in small tins.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Parish Council Meeting. A meeting of the Grappenhall Parish Council was held on Tuesday evening,when there were present : Messrs. J. C. Parr (presiding), Wareing, Warburton, Spence, J. Bradford, Ellison, T. Smith, Avery, Rath bone, Jenkins, and S. J. Woodcock (clerk). The Council decided to offer no objection to the plans for the proposed Warrington to Knutsford light railway. The Clerk was, however, instructed to write to the County Council pointing out the extreme narrowness of the bridge at the end of Cliffe-lane.—The Clerk was instructed to write informing Mr. F. R. Roberts that as he had not signed his declaration of office he was no longei qualified to be a member of the Council. St. Mary's Dramatic Society. No greater evidence need be expected of the remarkable degree of popularity achieved by the St. Mary's Society than the number of persons who turn up year after year to enjoy the performances. Last week some 3,143 persons witnessed "Belphegor," and the total number present at all the productions of the Society since 1900 has now grown to 17,966—a really fine record. All concerned in bringing about such a result are to be cordially congratulatal. The Hon. Secretaries wish us to notify to all holders of unused tickets that it would be a material help if these were returned in the addressed envelopes as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>, •,' tw' • / „ • •NI - - • " ,` • 4 „Zgir.o. , „ , ! ,•;, r"'"" • ' ' Ackt.</p>
        <p>New Course of Lectures. The Education Committee have made arrano-ements for a course of six lectures to be given by P-rofessor S. J. Chapman, M.A., of the Victoria University, Man- chester, on " Social questions of the present time." The lectures will be given on alternative Fridays, commencing on the 20th January next, in the Co-opera- tive Hall, the headings of the lectures being as follows, ' viz :-1, social questions, their nature and develop- ment ; 2, trade unionism; 3, methods of settling disputes ; 4, town life ; 5, co-opera,tion ; 6, the social outlook and social ideals. In view of the interest that the lectures will have to the general public the charge for admission to the lectures has been reduced to 2d; for each lecture. A Tour Through Ireland. This formed the subject of an interesting lecture which was (-riven by Mr. F. L. Crilly, Organizer of the United Irish League, at a meeting of the Warrington Branch of the League, which was held in the Winwick- street Co-operative Hall, last evening. The lecture was made additionally interesting by reason of its being extensively illustrated by a series of lantern slides depicting Ireland's famous beauty spots, Irish celebrities, and eviction scenes, the work of the "crowbar brigade" being shown under the latter heading. The lecture was interspersed with several national songs, given by Mr. J. Rabbitt, Mrs. Toole, Miss Sexton, and Miss Chicot (Manchester). Members of the Catholic League, Manchester, gave an exhibition of jig and reel dancing, and contributed their snare to a highly successfal entertainment.</p>
        <p>V-43N</p>
        <p>St John's Literary and Social Society. The usual fortnightly meeting of this Society was held in St. John's schoolroom on Tuesday evening,the President, Mr. P. 11. Brown. occupying the chair Mr. W. E.Rees read an intereeting paper on "Hands." There were many ways' Mr. Rees said, of treating such a sub jeet. Though some might dispute it, he was satisfied that man only, as distinguished from the lower animals, was possessed of hands. The hand was one of the marks of man's rank and power, and with it he conquered nature. The hand gave form and reality to the dreams and visions of man's brain and - soul. With it the thinker put his thoughts into written words, the musician interpreted on his instrument the marvellous harmonies that stirred the heart to its depths, and so on with the painter, &amp;c. They should all seek to train their hands to do their -very best, bringing out arid developing all that God had given them in their hands. They should do nothing slovenly, negligently or hurriedly, thus spoiling the workmanship. Their hands should be loyal and never withheld from duty.. Some discussion took place on the paper, and at the close Mr. Rees was accorded a hearty vote .f thanks. Woolcombers Ltd.—A Big Undertaking. We would draw the attention of our readers to the abridged prospectus published in our advertising columns, of Woolcoinbers, Limited—a company which has been formed to acquire, as on and from the 31st December' 1901, the entire undertaking and goodwill of the Yorkshire Woolcombers' Association, Limited, and to carry on the business. It should be explained that the Association was formed in 1899 for the pur- pose of amalgamating the woolcombing business of 38 companies and firms (some of which have been dis- Ipospd of or otherwise dealt with) and carries on a lArge business. It was, however, over-capitalised, and this ' initial misfortune was immediately succeeded by adverse trade conditions mainly consequent upon the drought in Australia. The Association was also embarrassed by a portion of its working capital having been devoted to the acquisition of patents and of other mills not included in the original amalgama- tion. The result was that in November, 1902, a receiver nnd manager was appointed on behalf of the debenture stock-holders of the Association, and such ereiver has since carried on the business. The reduc- tion effected in the capitalisation of the new company amounts to no less than £1,310,821 Notwithstanding adverse conditions, the accountants. certificate shows that during the years 1900-1904 the Association made an average annual profit of £51,813 13s. sd. (after charging administration expenses, but before providing for debenture, loan or other interest, executive directors' remuneration, income tax or depreciations). The new company acquires important patent rights, and takes over the property entirely free from the litigation which arose in connection witla the promotion of the old Association. Under an agree- ment entered into with the principal top makers connected with the Association it is guaranteed that for ten years the profits derived from the company's business shall be a least sufficient to pay the interest on the present issue of debenture stock. Other arrangements have been entered into to secure the co-operation of the top makers, and the sending of their wool to the new company. The share capital is fixed at £300,000, in 250,000 7 per cent.cumulative preference shares of £I each and 50,000 ordinary shares of the same amount. In addition, there are to be 41 per cent. first mortgage e.ebentures to the value of £325,000. There are now offered for subscription 210,000 preference shares and the whole amount of the debenture stock. The sub- scription list closes on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>THE CLOSING OF SHOPS. The members of the Warrington Grocers Association have decided to close all their shops on the Monday and Tuesday after Christaaas day and on the Monday after New Year's Day. - -</p>
        <p>RAILWAY ARRANGEMENTS. The London and North-Western and Cheshire Lines Railway Companies as will be seen from our advertising columns, announce that special excursions will be run uring the Xmas holidays.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VISIT TO PRESTON.—Mr. Thomas Ison Oculist and Aurist (of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary Leeds, established 1871), will visit the Castle Hotel,. Preston, Friday, December 23rd, hours 11 to 5.30., and may be consulted on all cases of failing sight and hear- ing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consul- tation. Spectacles adapted for weak or defective sight. Those unable to avail themselves of a personal consul- tation should write to Leeds for printed form of questions to answer, forwarded free. Letters to be addressed to 71, GREAT GEORG E-gTREET, Leeds.—Advt.</p>
