      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>• XMAS PRESIMS.—Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street,Warrington.Splendid selection.—Advt. Dispute About a Guarantee. _ .</head>
        <p>• XMAS PRESIMS.—Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street,Warrington.Splendid selection.—Advt. Dispute About a Guarantee. _ .</p>
        <p>Al the Warrington County Court on Thursday, be- fore his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., Mrs. Brophy, executrix of the late Councillor John Brophy sued Mrs. F. R. Copeland, of Dublin, for a guarantee of rent on the house 44, Crisfield street, Warrington. Mr. Madden, barrister, (instructed by Mr. J. R. Evans) appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. E. A. White de- fended. —The case for the plaintiff was that Mrs. Copeland took the house under a lease for five years and gave a guarantee that she would be responsible for the rent (.£25 a year) for that period. It was, however arranged that Mr. Kent, who with his family was living with Mrs. Copeland, should be named as the tenant in the lease, in order that he might retain his Parliamentary vote. Mrs. Copeland paid the rent. Having to leave Warrington in consequence of delicate health Mrs. Cope land agreed with Mr. Joseph Buckley, of Lower Walton, that he should become the tenant on under- taking to fulfil the conditions of the lease. A new lease Was prepared but Mr. Buckley, although re- peatedly approached, refused to sign it. Subse- quently he complained that the house was untenant- able and left it, declining to find another tenant. Plaintiff now sued in order that by the terms of the guarantee Mrs. Copeland should be responsible for the rent until the expiration of the five years—For the defence Mr. White contended that there had been a surrender of lease and that Mr. Buckley was tenant direct of the late Mr, Brophy and not a sub-tenant of Mrs. Copeland, as was shown by the fact that when Mr. Buckley gave notice Mrs. Brophy sent him a window bill that the house was to let, and advertised it in the local papers. Moreover, it was admitted that the gate was pad-locked by Mrs. Broph, so that assuming Mrs. Copeland was still liable for the rent she was locked out of the house.— His Honour held that as Mr. Buckley never signed a new lease no surrender took place, and Mrs. Copeland's liability was unaffected.—The result is that the defendant will be liable for the rent from the time that the house became untenanted until the five years has expired. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
