      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DECIMBEIR 17th, 1904.</p>
        <p>LORD GEORGE MR. BALFOUR'S TACTICS. HAMILTON is e4sily</p>
        <p>pleased. He has informed his late constituents at Ealing that he regards Mr. BALFOUR'S historic pronouncement at Edinburg as a sheet anchor of Free Trade. He has solemnly enjoined his successor in the affec_ tions of the Ealing Unionists never, never to desert Mr. BALFOUR, and his successor has cheer- fully givan that pledge. It must ne admitted that there is some force in the suggestion of the" Times" that if Lord GEORGE had waited a little longer for the exposition of Mr. BALFOUR'S economic views he need not have quitted the Cabinet. There is, indeed, very little to choose between the Edinburgh speech and the famous pamphlet on "Insular Free Trade ", with which Mr. BALFOUR regaled his colleagues before the split in his Cabinet. The blessed formula of Retaliation is the natural sequel of that production. Lord GEORGE may think that if he and others had not left the Government the Prime Minister's views would have inclined more strongly to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy. On the other hand it may be argued that if the Unionist Free Traders had stayed in the Cabinet they would have kept Mr. BALFOUR to the point of his Edinburgh speech just the same, This academic issue may have some inter- est for Lord GEORGE HAMILTON'S political friends; but the practical consideration at the present moment is that he is in perfect harmony with the gentleman who will stand tor Ealing at the General Election as a supporter of the Government. He believes that Mr. BALFOUR has definitely repudi- ated Protection ; and that the blessed formula o Retaliation, if is should ever be pat into practice, will not involve the levy of protective cluties.Some- bodyhas said that the future isronly the past entered by another gate. The futute Fiscal policy of the Unionist party will be the old Protection ; but, as the new gate is to be called Retaliation, Lord GEORGE HAMILTON is apparently quite happy. The " Times " is surprised by Mr. ASQUITH'S remark at Preston that the Edinburgh speech was "only another move in the game of procrastina- tion." "He might find it difficult," our contem- porary adds with really exquisite simplicity," to shew where procrastination comes in, seeing that Mr. BALFOUR has from the first declared that the</p>
      </div>
