      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>THERE IS ONLY ONE,</head>
        <p>THERE IS ONLY ONE,</p>
        <p>There is only one "First" in a race, and it is acknow- ledged without doubt that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, is the Best Remedy of the Age for Weakness, Nervousness, Indigestion, Loss of Appetite, Chest Affections, Low Spirits, and Influenza. This remedy of world-wide fame strengthens that part of the system which is weakest or has been weakened by disease, and therefore more liable to colds and their attendant ailments; it purifies the blood and stimulates the circulation, assists and promotes diges- tion, and improves the appetite. It braces the nerves and fortifies the muscles, rouses the sluggish liver, and thus enlivens the spirits; it removes all impurities and obstructions from the human body, and Oyes tone to the whole system. Gwilym Evans' Gumine Bitters is purely vegetable, for the formula ,--ncliicles the Tonic properties of Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Burdock, Lavender, and Dandelion, in addition to Quinine, and is suitable to all ages, from the infant to the adult, and confidently recommended to those who have to devote themselves to study and brain work, to all who work long hours in close rooms, to those who breathe impure air, and all who have to stand exposure of the weather. People that suffer from brain fag and sleeplessness, who feel "out of sorts " and " run down," find rapid and permanent relief from the use of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. The occupations of some men keep their whole faculties continually at their furthest stretch, and life is all hurry and worry. Little wonder it is that this extreme tension leads to an unusual wear and tear of the nerves and that these hard-worked toilers require an extra sustenance for body and brain. Now, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters feeds both, and men are wise if they look ahead and preserve their strength by taking this infallible feeder to all vital powers. There are five fixed facts about this renowned preparation Ist, used by the medical profession, 2nd, a purely vege- table compound ; 3rd, absolutely free from mercury; 4th, tested for a quarter of a century; sth, The Best Tonic. There are many medicinal tonics offered to the public, but they may be classified under two heads : The( Best and The Rest. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is the best. Caution, beware of imitations. See that you get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, with the name "Gwilym Evans "on label, stamp and bottle. Sold everywhere in bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d., or in cases containing three 4s. 6d. bottles at 12s. 6d. per case. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward for the above prices carriage free. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BREACH OF THE FACTORY ACT. At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Messrs. John Walker and Company, proprietors of the "Warrington Examiner," were summoned for employing eleven young women contrary to the Factory Act, on Friday night, November 25th.—Mr. Augustus Lewis, H.M. inspector of factories, said that he and Inspector Eatock visited the defendants' works at 10.35 on the night in question and found eleven young women engaged, principally in setting type for the newspaper. Mr. Walker, the editor, told him that the girls glad been kept after ten o'clock because A the report of a big meeting which took place that night, having to be set for the paper. The defend- ants had been fined ss. and costs previously fora similar offence respecting a boy.—Mr. E. V. Walker represented the defendant company, and said the case was an excep- tional one, and they were obliged to keep the girls working after ten owing to the Liberal meeting which did not end until nearly ten. Their paper was a Liberal organ, and they had to produce a report in the next morning's papers. The offence for which they were fined previously was only a technical one and was not on all fours with the present prosecution,as it related to the employment of a boy.—The Bench imposed a fine of £1 and costs in five of the cases, and the Factory Inspector withdrew the other six on payment of costs. —The fines and costs amounted in all to £9 Bs. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>AT DEATH'S DOOR.</head>
        <p>AT DEATH'S DOOR.</p>
        <p>AN EXAMPLE OF THE HEALING POWER OF SEAWEED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. LEE, 53, Ne wland-street, Wes to Lincoln,writes:— " 1 have been suffering for over 18 months with a weak heart and stomach and kidney disease. I had taken all sorts of medicine. My neighbours all thought I was going to die—l thought so myself, for I never expected to live to be 54. For my present healthy condition I have to thank Vmso's SEAWEED Toxic, it has bgen worth more than gold to me. I feel it my duty —to recommend it, for I was for months and could not keep anything on my stomach ; now I can eat anything, and my friends are all asking what it is that has done me no much good ; I tell them VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC" VENO'S SEA WEED TONIC is now the great popular medicine for stomach, liver, kidney and blood diseases. Cures permanently. No return. You feel better immediately. is. Id. &amp; 23.95.,at Chemists everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ELECTION AMENITIES.</head>
        <p>ELECTION AMENITIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Howes Sues for Libel.</p>
        <p>A DISCREDITABLE TORY LEAFLET.</p>
        <p>On Monday, in London, Mr. Justice Wills and a special jury had before them the case of Howes v. Packer, which was an action brought by Mr. Joseph Howes a lecturar and nlember of the Methodist body in Rochdale, to recover damages for alleged libel from Mr. Tom Herbert Packer, an agent on the Conservative side during the last Mid-Devon Parliamentary Elec- tion, when Mr. Eve, K.C.L., was elected. Mr. George Wallace, in opening plaintiff's case, said the alleged libel was distributed at the close of a meet- ing which the plaintiff had addressed the night before the polling, and which was as follows : Before believing a word told you by the Radical speaker. Joseph Howes, ask him if he is the Joseph Howes who was once a lecturer on Atheism, and whose lecture was entitled "A hunt after the Devil !" Ask him if he is the Joseph Howes who was so distrusted by the electors of Morecambe, where he lived, that at an election there he was at the bottom of the poll, three other candidates being above him? Ask him if he is the same Joseph Howes, whose glaring and reck- less mis-statements made at Radical meetings in different parts of the country have been refuted over and over again. Don't be misled by the glib oratory of such men, but vote for Harrison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LECTURER AND PREACHER.</head>
        <p>LECTURER AND PREACHER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes, continued Mr. Wallale, was 48 years of age. He was a Methodist by religion, and for the last 20 years had lectured on various subjects, including religion and politics, in various parts of the country. He was trustee of a Methodist chapel in Lancashire,and preached a great di:SMA was true that some 20 years ago he called himself a Secularist, but he certainly never was an Atheist, as alleged by defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PLAINTIFF IN THE BOX.</head>
        <p>PLAINTIFF IN THE BOX.</p>
        <p>Plaintiff admitted that 20 years ago he wrote a pamphlet upon" Hunting the devil." As to the election at Morecambe, it appeared that as a publican had been put up for the Central Ward of Morecambe Urban Dis- trict Council some enthusiastic friends of witness thought they would" run him " for the other party. What was the result ?—Well I lost 14y four votes. That is all. I deny that I ever ran away when con- fronted with certain statements I had made in the course of my Radical lectures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TEIE DEFENCE.</head>
        <p>TEIE DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>Dafendant's counsel submitted there was no case to answer. The Aid-Devon election was one in which som 3 halt was shown by supporters of both candidates, but certainly the wording of the leaflet could not be 13 Ili ta na ground for an action like the present. The jury found for defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BRUTALITY AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>BRUTALITY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, James Carroll (22), a labourer was charged with having at Widnes, on October =, feloniously unlawfully and maliciously caused grievous bodily harm to his sister-in-law, Grace Patience Mahar, aged fourteen. Mr. D'Arcy prosecuted and Mr. Segar appeared on behalf of the prisoner.—The prosecutrix related how the prisoner meeting her in an entry, chased her home. Later in the evening he came to the house shouting that he would fight anybody in it. Witness opened the door, when he seized her by the throat, dragged her out, and threw her down in the street, her head striking the pavement. As a result she remained in hospital for eleven days. Daring that time she was occasionally unconscious.—Mr. Seger said the man came to the house to demand that his wife should be sent out. The Mahar family were waiting for him with pokers and sticks, and he contended that the girl was knocked down in the melee.—Prisoner was found guilty, awl sentenced to eighteen months' imprison- ment.</p>
      </div>
