      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRA_PHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRA_PHS.</p>
        <p>" WHAT a lovely waste basket the editor has!" "Yes, he's so kind-hearted, he means the poet's lines shall fall in pleasant places." MISTRESS (arranging for the dinner): "Didn't the grocer send the macaroni ?" Cook: "Yes, mum, but Oi sent it back. Every one of them stims wuz empty." "Wiry do you call me birdie, my dear?" in- quired a wife of her husband. "Because," was the answer, "you are always associated in my mind with a bill." MANAGER (to actor): "In the new piece you will die in the fifth act. There will be, a funeral song for three voices, and as we have' only two singers you will have to help them out." KNEW THE SRQUEL.—MTS. Grinkam "John, I think you might as well get ready to go for the doctor." Grinkam : "Who's ill now?" Mrs. Grinkham : "No one yet; but I node° that mother's just found a new patent medicine almanack and is reading the page of symptoms."</p>
        <p>HE'D BEEN THERE. —Little Ethel: "I wonder why Adam and Eve had such an awful time just because they ate one little apple?" Johnny (reflectively): "Perhaps it wasn't ripe." "You ought to do something for that °old of yours. A neglected oold often leads to serious co:lsequences." This cold isn't neglected. Everybody 1 know is lookin' after it."</p>
        <p>"trx iirown-Joneses don't speak to us any more." "What's the trouble?" "Well, we locked up and went off to the country Saturday, ant left their Sunday dinner in our icechest." Nor long ago in a school a smart little girl on being asked by her teacher to explain the meaning of the word "sweetheart" promptly replied: "Please, ma'am, itr's a thing for courtin' with." THE BETT HE COULD Do.—" Jenkins wanted a job with light work and regular pay." " Yes ; aril he got a job with regular work and light pay." A PROHIBITED LuxITRY. —Mrs. Henpeck (acridly) : "So Mr. Dusnap did not even offer you a thir.king part in the amateur theatricals?" Mr. Henpeck (wearily): "Weil, he knew you would never let me play it." MERITORIOUS. —Gokisby : " Your new son-in- law made a good thing by his marriagQ, old man." Skadds : " Yes 2 • but eventually he'd have become a rich man, anyhow. The way he worked to get his wife convineed- me of that.'</p>
        <p>MRS. NEWWED (handing tramp several biscuits) : "Here, my poor man, are some of my home-made biscuits, You will find the saw and axe in the woodshed." Tramp (closely examining the bis- cuits) : "Are they as bad as that, mum?" NOT A SPECIALIST. —A Scotch minister from a large town preaching in a rural parish was asked to pray for rain. He did so, and the rain came in floods and destroyad some of the crops; where- upon one elder remarked to another, "This comes ot entrusting sic a request to a meenister who isna acqnentit wi farming." OPEN TO DISCUSSION. —Scrivener : "I've just finished the novel that will be the most.talked-of book of the year." Friend "What makes you so sure it will be successful ?" Scrivener: "Why, I've got a title for it that there are nine different ways of pronouncing!"</p>
        <p>Skin 'EXASPERATING.—Our wives have their own troubles. Think how they must feel when they bring in, for our inspection, a new hat, about which they have worried for weeks, and we exclaim, "Is that a new hat?"</p>
        <p>CHOPPING HIM —Borrowby (effusively) : "Ho ! Grimehaw, is this really you, old man? Haven't seen you for an age. Thought you were dead, don't you know, and " Grimshaw : ".Just keep on thinking so, Borrowby I" CAUSE FOR APPREHENSION. —" afraid I in- sulted Mrs. Newmate at the baby-party yesterday." " How ? " "I told her the baby looked like her." "That wouldn't insult her; %would delight her." "That's what I thought; but I've learned since that the baby wasn't hers." "Dons your husband work, Mrs. Dashley ? " "Indeed he does not. He plays from one year's end to the other." "How can he do that and keep the wolf from the door?" "Mr. Dashley is a musician."</p>
        <p>How HE tIOT HIS NAME.—" What did you say was the name of your horse?" "Nail." "Nail?' "Yes, Nail—N.:ad-1." "What induced _you to give the animal such a queer name?" "Nail is the most appropiatename imaginable. My wife can't drive him home."</p>
        <p>A PROMINENT 'riothall club was having a test match with a view to discovering the merits of a new goalkeeper. After the game a supporter of the club accosted a member of the committee as to how the new man had acquitted himself. " Yon's the man," replied the committeeman. "He lets nothing past _but the wind." _ _</p>
        <p>HEAB -OF FIRM : " What do you mean, sir, by going to steep at your desk in broad daylight?' Clerk: "I bag a thousand pardons, sir, but my baby kept me awake all last night, and 1 am dead tired." Head of Firm: "Oh, well, you might bring the child to business to-morrow, so that you may keep awake during the day as well." MISTRESS: "You say you are well recommended?" Maid: "Indeed, ma'am, I have thirty-nine excellent references from ladies' I have worked for.' Mistress: "How long have you been in domestic service?" Maid "Two years, ma'am." OLD GENTLEMAN: "'My boy, don't. you go to school ? " Boy; "Yes, sir. " It's long after nine, and here you are playing." "That's all right. We had rather a late breakfast, and mamilia was afraid I'd be late, so she wrote me an excuse, and I've got it in my pocket." Par: "Waiter, I can't drink this &amp;yap." The waiter took it away accordingly and 'brought a fresh plateful. "I can't drink this either," said Pat. So the waiter summoned the chef, who asked what was the matter with the soup. "There is nothing the matter with it," was the answer, "but I have no spoon." ATTORNEY: "You say you had called to see Miss Billings, and were at the house at the time the burglary was oommitted ? " Witness: "Yes, sir." "Then how did it happen that when the prisoner dashed into the room and assaulted you, you leaped through the window and Went home making no attempt to defend the lady or give th; alarm?" "I thought it was her father." "So," he said, severely, "you have aocepted that young fool, have you?" "To all intents and purposes, papa," she admitted, freely. " You ought to know bette; than that," he grumbled. "Don't you know that he plays cards for money, and bets on horse races ?" Yes, papa." And his salary is too small to support a wife." "I know it, papa." "Well, he does not expect me to support him, does he ? " She smiled softly. "No," she hesitated, "but he says if you'll only keep on playing cards with him three nights in the week, there won't be any trouble about our getting along beautifully." _</p>
        <p>BErs(; wise man, he desired to take no risks. "Of course you understand," he said, by way of preface, "that I have plenty of female relatives." "Certainly," she answered, somewhat nonplussed. "I have four sisters already," he wen on. "1 realise all that," she returned, "but I ail to see how it interests me." 'Only indirect y," he said. "Before saying what I have to say, I merely desire to have it understood that I have my full quota of relatives of that description. , Do I make myself clear?" "I think I gras your meaning," she answered. "In that case,", he announced. "I will ask you to be my wife." ... . . ._ _</p>
        <p>THE A.IIT CrF RiPORTING.— Interviewer : "I have come to get your views on the proposed change in the curriculum of the school." Mr. Swelhead : "Curriculum ! What's that mean? I'm against it, whatever it is." Mr. Swelhead, reading the report of the interview in the next morniti4's papers " g Ohr distinguished townsman, Mr. M. T. Swelhead, was found' at his charming home surrounded by abundant indications of ripe scholarship and sturdy common sense. In reply to our representative's questions, he said: "I do not desire to force my opinions on the public, but this I will say, that I have given to this question long and studious attention, incidentally examining upon the curricula of ins:itutions of learning, both at home and abroad, and, although I found in the existing course of study not a few matters for ,-ondemnation, still, I cannot say that I should advise any radical' change until I have further time to examine into the subject.'" "By George, that fellow's got my exact language, word for word! And he didn't take note neither! Jimminy, but what a memory that follow must have!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TREE AND SALE OF WORK.—The annum Christmas tree and sale of plain work in connection with St. Thomas's Church was held in the Church In- stitute on Saturday, and was opened by Mrs. Henry Greenall. There were the usual attractions and the sale proved very successful, £2.1 oq. lid. being realized. The proceeds are devoted to the Society for the Propa- gation of the Gospel and the Church of England Society for providing Homes for Waifs and Strays.</p>
      </div>
