      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, Decemb:r 17, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Decemb:r 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDIJCATIT: COMMITTEE Business of the Next Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly' meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will be held at the Town flail on Monday evening next,when the following amongst other business will be disposed of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>RESIGNATION,</head>
        <p>RESIGNATION,</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee received sad accepted the resignations of Miss Shaw, hmdmistress, Fairfield infants school, and Mrs. M. H. Hudson, uncertificated assistant mistress, Silver-street Wesleyan infants school. With regard ,to Miss Shaw the com- mittee resolved "Mist the committee place on record their high appreciation of the long and successful services rendered by her to the cause of education in the town, and express the hope that she may be long spared to enjoy the rest she so well deserves. The committee also decided to accept the following. resignations :—Miss Charlotte A. Quine. certificated assistant, Hamilton-street, girls; Miss Ada Chadwick supplementary assistant, Heathside, infants; Mrs. Anna Bell, certificated assistant, Warrington Parochial, infants.</p>
        <p>APPOINMIENT6.</p>
        <p>The School Management Cirnmitte-.? recommend the appointment of Miss Mary k Warburton in succession to Miss Shaw as headmistress of Fairfield infants, Miss M. A. Muirhead, assistant mistress, St. Ann's National School, and Miss A. F. Cowgill, assistant mistress, St James' School. The committee also resolved that consent be given to the appointment of Miss Caroline Sims as a certificated assistant mistress in the mixed department of the St. James' school.Latchford, at a commencing salary at the rate of £B5 per annum.</p>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF FIIRFIELD SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A letter was submitted to the School Management Committee from the managers of the Fairfield school, intimating that they are willing to entertain the sug-. gestion made to them by the Sub-Committee as to the conversion of one or both of the girls' departments of the school into an upper standard school but that they would like to be informed as to the detailed arrange- ments before coming to any definite conclusion. The principle of the proposed conversion of one of those departments into an upper standard school was approved by the committee and the arrangements in reference thereto were left in the hands of the sub- committee. BONUSES FOR HEID TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>A letter was submitted from the managers of the St. Mary's school,applying for the sum due to the Headmaster Athe Boys'department and the Headmistress of the Girls' department of that school in respect of two periods of six months during the year ended 31st October. 1901, in. which the average attendance of those departments ex- ceeded the maximum of the class in which they were placed for the purposes of the scale of salaries, and it was resolved that the sum of £2O be awarded to the Headmaster of the Boys' department, and £2 10s, to the Headmistress of the Girls' department.</p>
        <p>THE POSITION OF THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL,</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE'S RECOMMENDATIONS.</p>
        <p>A letter, dated the 19th November, 1904, was sub- mitted to the School Attendance Committee, from the governers of the Warrington Grammar school intimat- ing that they consider that the committee's pro- posals in regard to the school should be first placed before the Board of Education before the governors express their views upon them. It was there upon resolved that a reply be sent to the letter, dated the 20th August, 1904, from the Board of Education—which suggested an amendment of the scheme for the government of the school and a contri- bution from the Committee to its funds—embodying the following observations :--(l)':the Committee are of opinion that the Local Authority ought not to grant any financial aid to the Gramm school until the school has been placed under the inspection of and has been duly recognised by the Board of Education; (2) the committee will be willing to recommend the Local Authority to grant such financial aid as may be necessary to make and maintain the " Boteler ' Grammar school as an efficient secondary school,on the following conditions (a) that the governing body of the school should be so re-constituted that a majority of its members shall be representatives of the Local Authorities—the County Borough of Warrington, and the Counties of Lancashire and Cheshire; (b) that there should be some reduction in the maximum fees charged, and it is suggested that such maximum fee should be £9, and the minimum £6; (c) tkat greater encouragement should be given to boys seeking admission to the school from the elementary schools at the age of 12,and from the Secondary school of the Local Authority at the age of 14 years; (d) that the appointment, dismissal, and determination of the staff of the school shall be vested in the governors; and (e) that there should be a modification of the terms of the present Headmaster's remunerations, as, in view of the expected increase of scholars under the improved methods,the existing arrangements, especially in respect of capitation grants, would be inequitable.</p>
        <p>FEES AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A letter, dated the 10th November, 1904, was submit- ted from the Board of Education, with reference to the Committee's application for the continued recognition of the Secondary school, and asking that the fee of ninepence per week, 10s. 6d. per term, and 30s. per annum, should be increased to not less than £3 per annum, and that exemption from such fee should be granted to not more than 25 per cent. of the total num- ber of scholars in theschool ; and that the Head Master should be allowed to exercise the power of appointment and dismissal of the assistant teachers in the school, subject to the approval of the governors. It was decided that a reply be sent to the effect that the amount of the fee was fixed after very careful considera- tion of the local circumstances, and that any increase therein would result in ,a considerable reduction in the number of children in the school ; and that the com- mittee are not prepared to grant powers to the Head master of the school which they do not delegate to any other of their officers,tand that the appointment and dismissal of assistant teachers must rest with them.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS SA.LARIES.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee recommended that as the attendance at St. I3enedict's school was below the minimum average, the Wary o the held- master be not increased. The committee appointed Rev. N. Wilson, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. J. Upson, and the Chairman of the Committee to consider and report to the committee as to whether the operation of the scale of salaries for head teachers can be improved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>E DUC C ON FE RENC E.</head>
        <p>E DUC C ON FE RENC E.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommend that Mr. Aide man Bennett, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. J. Upson, and the director be appointed to represent the committee at the third annual meeting of the North of England education conference to be held at Liver- pool on the 6th and 7th January, 1905. The committee also recommend that the railway fares of not more than 20 assistant teachers, desiring to attend the con- ference, be paid by the committee,</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATT&amp;NDANCE COMMITTEE. THE CLOSING or SCHOOLS FOR BAZAARS AND TEA PARTIES.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee have resolved that the managers of the schools be requested to arrange for all bazaars, tea parties, &amp;c., to take place during the holidays; and that a school must not be closed for these purposes until application has been made to the corn- mittee and their sanction obtained.</p>
        <p>THE TEA.CHING OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>A communication, dated the 25th November, 1904, was submitted from Sir William Broadbent, on behalf of the committee of the medical profession of the United Kingdom constituted to promote the teaching of hygieneandltemperance in public elementary schools, asking the committee to support endeavour, by means of a petition to Parliament, and also by provision being made for the training of the teachers in their schools to qualify them to give such instruction: . . . . . .</p>
        <p>The comniittee felt that he introduction of the com- pulsory teaching of these subjects into the curriculum of their schools would not be as effective asthe instruc- tion which is being constantlyand spontaneously given, and it was accordingly decided not to take the action suggested in the matter.</p>
        <p>VISITS TO INDUSTRIAL SCHOOLS. WARRINGTON CHILDREN SMALL FOR THEIR AGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Moore Murray presented a report on visi ts paid to Industrial schools in which he says "All the boys appear to be well cared for, but the deputation could not help being impressed by the fact that the majority of the Warrington boys in the schools were very small for their age. On inquiry they found that this was not unusual, and it would appear that both the physical and mental development have been stunted by neglect during early childhood i The chief concern of the committee s the ultimate. , disposal of the children they send to the Industrial schools. Four boys have been discharged, from the institution this year, of whom three are known to be in good employment, and promise to become useful mem- bers of society. The great difficulty to be contended with is the return of the children to their former homes. The committee can understand the desire of the child to return, and in some instances the wish of the parents for their return. The deputation has found on enquiry at the schools that in scarcely one instance do the parents of the children communicate with them during the whole time of their being in the schools. The various governors of the schools toldus that it was only at the expiration of the boys' term of commitment that the parents did take any interest in them, and the only conclusion that could be drawn was that this interest was awakened by the fact that the boy bad reached an age at which he would become a wage earner; there are exceptions to this but they are few. In many cases there had been no improvement in the homes of the parents, aiikl to send the children back to such surroundings would be to undo the work of years. It was found that most of the governors were en- deavouring to keep in touch with the children after they have left school. In future, the governors of the schools will be asked to notify the committee whenever it is proposed to discharge a child. Enquiries will be then made so as to enable the Governors of the Institu- tion to decide as to what shall be the ultimate dis- posal of the child."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ALLEGED SERIOUS WIFE_ ASSAULT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED SERIOUS WIFE_ ASSAULT.</p>
        <p>lAt Widnes, on Monday, Charles Matthews, labourer, , was committed tcv the quarter sessions for trial,charged with a serious assault upon his wife.</p>
      </div>
