      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>FOR WORKING MB.</head>
        <p>FOR WORKING MB.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR</p>
        <p>Author of 'Search Lights on. Land, Labour</p>
        <p>and Social -Problems."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PRICE OF SUGAR,</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF SUGAR,</p>
        <p>Commerce will only flourish when it is let alone and when no external considerationsAare allowed tt• impede the free sale and exchange of commodities. You cannot corner a commoditylof universal use while you have a free and open market. It is when markets are artificially restricted that corners and combina- tions to raise prices spring up like fungus growths.— "The Spectator." I know no finer examples of the blessings of Free Trade than of the growth of our sugar industries. We do not grow a single ounce of sugar in this country, but we know how to use it when it is grown, so the enter- prising foreigner has for years been supplying all our needs at less than a penny per pound.</p>
        <p>1.-CHEAP SUGAR HAS CREATED MANY NEW INDUSTRIES.</p>
        <p>Under the old order of things we obtained our supplies of cane sugar from the West Indies, and it was refined at Bristol, and Greenock, and other towns in the United Kingdom, But beet sugar could be raised so cheaply that it captured our markets and enabled our manufacturers to embark inall sorts of new enterprises. The Frys of Bristol, and the Ctdburys of Birmingham, and the Rowntrees of York began making cocoa and chocolate with this cheap sugar, and employing thousands of hands. Mr. Fry tells me they have seven large factories in the city of Bristol, employing 4,000 workpeople. Mr. Hirtley, of Aintree, and Mr. Reiner, of Dundee, and ever so many other enterprising men built big factories for the manufacture of marmalade and jam. Theß our fruit preservers, and our confec- tioners, and our mineral water bottles, and our biscuit makers followed their example, and they not oaf y supplied all our needs, but they exported their products to the ends of the earth.</p>
        <p>I can remember the day when sugar was sixpence per pound, and it was as coarse and heavy as wet sand. The poor man's child hardly ever tasted sugar in those days and he was thankful if he could get a bit of black treacle, as bitter as soot, instead of butter to his bread. In those times of cruel taxation, cocoa factories, and confectionery works, and biscuit manufactories were unknown, and most of our modern luxuries in which sugar plays a part could not be had for love or money. How did our sugar industries take root and grow? By the simple progress of letting our trade alone. Free Trade gave us an abundance of cheap sugar,a,nd we were free to use it as we pleased when we got it. So we started on an era of industrial enterprise that found profitable employment for more than one hundred thousand of our people in new trades and callings. The Tariff Reformers tell us that cheap sugar threw five thousand of the tfald refiners out of work, and drove them to other trades or to the workhouse. Well, if it did, we should welcome it when it finds work for twentrtimes as many people in other directions. Every new improvement hits the old order of things. Stage coaches ruined the pack horses, and railways disestab- lished the stage coaches, and electric tramcars have hit the railways, and these things will happen to the end of the chapter. You may judge of the magnitude of our sugar in- dustries from the fact that we consume about one- sixth of the whole world's sugar harvest, and that our consumption three years ago reached the enormous average of ninety-seven pounds per head for every man, woman,and child in the kingdom. This is a fine example of what open markets, and free trade, and cheap raw material can do to create new industries and to solve the problem of the unemployed.</p>
        <p>IL-THE RISE IN SUGAR IS A SPLENDID OBJECT LESSON FOR FREE TRADERS.</p>
        <p>I have shown yo g that so long as sugar was untaxed, and our markets were open and free, we could buy as much as we required for less thin a penny per pound. But when we taxed sugar a halfpenny per pound, and artificially restricted our supplies, we sent up the price more than a penny per pound to the consumer. If you want to know how much misery, and mischief, and poverty can be wrought by taking away our Free Trade arid compelling us to buy in dear markets yo u should study the history of the Sugar Convention, and note its effect on supplies and prices. Three years ago Hr. Chamberlain let us into the clumsiest booby trap that was ever set for catching fools and madmen. France,Gerraany, Belgium, Austria and one or tw) other nations ware tired of givi rig bounties to their own snit minufacturers for our benefit.</p>
        <p>These bounties, which were paid out of their National taxes, enabled their sugar refiners to tproduce enormous quantities of cheap sugar, and' dump it in this country, while they kept up high prices to our own people. The foreign governments got tired of this silly game, and wanted to end it. But they could not carry out their schemes unless we would bind ourselves to receive no more bounty fed sugar into this country, so they invited us to a Convention at Brussels to ratify this idiotic scheme. It was a fine game for the foreigner, because it relieved their taxes and reduced the prices to their own people. But it was rough on the Englishman, because it reduced his supplies, and raised his prices,and increased the cost of living, and struck a deadly blow to our sugar industries. Mr.' Chamberlain must have foreseen all this. and how any superannuated idiot could be dragged into such a one-sided bargain is more than I can tell.</p>
        <p>He says the law of supply and demand settles the price of sugar. That is just what I say, but I want to know why he cut off our supplies from Russia and Argentina, and all the other sugar producing countries that did not sign the Convention. He is the author of all the mischief, and he knows it. Probably he thinks this is another feather in his cap. Man for man we are the largest consumers of sugar in the world. Why should we refuse to buy any nation's supplies because they are too cheap? And why should Mr. Chamberlain pledge us to any wild-cat scheme that will injure us and benefit our foreign rivals. Well the truth is, Josephhas been thinking I -Imperially, and am sorry to say he has these fits of lunacy pretty frequently. He is under the delusion that cheap sugar is a bad thing for us, and it will ruin our West Indian planters, so he asks us to raise the price of our sugar by five pounds per ton for their benefit. They are sending us less than fifty thousand tons a year, so it would cost us less than a quarter of a million a year to save them from bank- ruptcy. If that little dole would have saved them, why didihe not give it to them as he gave his doles, and bribes, and sops to the landowners, and the parsons and the brewers? _ _ _</p>
        <p>He preferred the old Protectionist dodge of closing our markets. He not only shut out the bounty fed sugar from the nations that joined the Convention, but also from those nations that refused to sign it. Russia has half a million tons of cheap sugar, to offer us now, but we will not buy it. So she is selling it to the Swiss, and they are building factories to pick up our ruined industries.</p>
        <p>Now let us see the immediate effects of this Conven- tion. In the first place, it enables the nine nations who signed it to reduce the cost to their own pegple by fifty per cent. or more. And it raises the pried in this country a half-penny per pound artificially by limiting our supplies and,anotker halfpenny by the sugar duty. So we are paying more titan a penny per pound than we ought to pay, while our rivals have their prices greatly reduced. This is "taxing the foreigner with a vengeance, and it is a good example of the new Brummagen patriotism. In the second place, it imposes an intolerable burden on our own people. In the wholesale market the price of sugar has risen from six shillings per hundredweight to more than fourteen shillings, and the retail price has gone up apenny or more per pound. So we are paying between eight and nine millions a year more for Our sugar than we were paying two or three years ago in the hope that we shall put a quarter of a million in- to the pockets of the West Indian planter. This is the new Imperialism, and it is about as sane and wise as the conduct of the fool who set fire to his house to roast his pig'. if any jabbering lunatic from Bedlam or Colney Hatch attempted to do such silly things as these, his keepers w.)uld shave his head awl put him in a strait jacket. But when our Protectionist madmen do these tricks we make them into Cabinet Ministers and give them one hundred pounds a week. ONow let me put these tell-tale figures into another form, that you may see their meaning more clearly and fully appreciate their significance. This rise in the price of sugar represents an extra sixpence per week added to the cost of living for every family in this country. Mr. W. S. Robson, M.P. puts the thing in a nutshell when he says: `• Undoubtedly the Brussels Convention has had the effect we predicted of both raising prices and increasing fluctuations of price. Every Christmas pudding in England will be made dearer' or smaller, or worse tbis vinter. That is the joint Christmas present of Balfour and ghamberlain to the English poor." Now I have shown you that the Sugar Convention has raised the price of raw material by one hundred and forty per cent. in this country. As a result of this rise in price our consumption has fallen from ninety-seven to seventy-nine pounds per head of our population, and our greatest sugar industries are'being ceippled by the increase in the cost of raw material. _ - _</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain has been rampaging up and down the country for a year and a half in search of" ruined industries," and lying for all he is worth. He can find some genuine ones now, and if he will tell the truth about them he will admit that they are samples of his own mischievous handiwork. In the East-end of London, within a stone's throw of the place where he Is to make his next speech, he will find a large confec- tionery factory which at one time employed seven or eight hundred men, women, and chil- dren. Now it is silent and deserted, and it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>SALE OF WORK AT GOLBORNE STREET. Big Building Scheme Contemplated.</head>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT GOLBORNE STREET. Big Building Scheme Contemplated.</p>
        <p>The members of the Golborne-street Baptist Church contemplate the erection during the next few years of a new Church and Sunday school, as it has long been felt that the present place of worship is not adequate to meet the demands of an ever-increasing congregation. The Baptist Union has been approached and has promised £5OO towards the cost of a new building, and the Rev. H. Dunnico is hopeful of inducing the Union to increase their offer to one of £l,OOl The trustees of the church have in hand £3OO and it is hoped to rise the balance of £l,OOO by a bazaar to be held in about eighteen months' time. For some time the members of the Church have bees working in preparation for the bazaar and latterly they have been considerably handicapped through lack of funds, and it was with the object of affording them some financial assistance that a sale of work was held in the Golborne-street schools on Thursday. The Rev. H. DIINI•TICO presided over the opening ceremony at which there was a large attendance. He was not, he said, an admirer of sales of work but rather regarded them as regrettable necessities, and he hoped the time would not be long in coming when Christian Churches would be able to raise their funds in other ways. (Hear, hear.) Mrs. C. SIMPsoN, who was cordially received, had pleasure in declaring the sale open. In a brief but interesting speech she strongly advocated greater unity amongst all Christian Churches.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Simpson was heartily thanked for her services, on the motion of Mr. FEW, seconded by Mr. CLA.T- WORTHY, and the sales commenced. The stalls, which contained a variety of useful and ornamental articles were in charge of the following ladies :—Married Ladies' stall : Mesdames Dunnico, Few, Clatworthy, Feamley, Freeman, G. Hall, and H. Buckley. Young Ladies' stall: Mrs. Rickers, Mrs. Davies, Miss Hawthorn and Miss Wood. Refreshment stall: The Misses Roberts, Hall and Harmer. Young Men's stall : Mr. Pepper an-I Mr. Birratt F. lasters, Mr. Buckley, Mr. T. Longshaw and Nliss Elankey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>HOLIDAY ARRANGEMENTS. ---- Glii,istmas Advance Posting,</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY ARRANGEMENTS. ---- Glii,istmas Advance Posting,</p>
        <p>The plan of posting letters in advance to be delivered on Christmas Day, has proved to be so successful here last year, that, it is intended not only to have recourse to the same system again this year, so far as Warrington is concerned, but, also to extend it to the Postal Rursl District subordinate to Warrington. The arrangement last year, afforded very great mile f to the work of the Post Office Staff, the public con- venience being also much increased thereby. As shewn in the notice which can be seen at the Post office, the period during which advance letters can be received, will this year be, from the 17th to the 23rd December—except Sunday 18th instant—inclusive. Those who desire to take advantage of the arrange- ment,are asked to hand in their letters at the earliest possible date, as, every'day in advance, brings so much more assistance to the Post office. _ _</p>
        <p>Attention is called to the conditions, that," Advance Letters" should not be posEed in Piliar-boxes, or in any Letter-boxes. but should be handed over the I Counter of a Post. afice as Advance Letters. No in- scription on them is required, and the mere fact of their being handed in over the Counter will make them Advance Letters, and, they will be stamped with a special stamp showing that they are to be delivered on Christmas Day. So far as any judgment can be formed beforehand there is likely to be a considerable increase of corres- pondence this year, and, it is to be hoped, that, the public will once more shew favour to the schema of advance posting." To find room for the increased work consequent on ,Christmas, the Post Office has wain taken the Gym- nasium, by the kind permission of the Corporation, for parcel post purposes, and, in addition this year, it has been judged necessary to engage the Assembly Rooms at the Conservative but), as an auxiliary for the sort- ing of letters. It should, perhaps, be made clear,-that, the experi- ment of advance posting will not include correspond- ence for other towns—the arrangement being a purely local one, for Warrington and its sub rdinate postal. district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>"SPORTING CHRONICLE" ANNUAL.</head>
        <p>THE "SPORTING CHRONICLE" ANNUAL.</p>
        <p>, The twenty-ninth issue of the "Sporting Chronicle Annual" is to hand. The volume for 1905 affords complete information on every important and popular branch of sport, and is arranged so as to be very easy of reference. To footballers, cricketers, boxers, fencers, gymnasts, weight-lifters swimmers, golfers, foot-racers, coursers, turfites, cyclists, lovers of billiards, and various other pastimes, the brochure is invaluable, as all the new records for the year are tabulated. Few arguments on matters of sport need remain undecided if the annual be consulted. This is theonly work of its kind. The cover presents a photo- graph of G. E. L3.rner, the new amateur champion walker, and the frontispiece consists of five medallion portraits of such celebrities as J. W. Morton, the English sprinter; George Wilkinson, the swimmer and water polo player: Leon Meredith, the wonderful cyclist ; Tom Reece, the billiard player; and T. F. Keane, the pedestrian. The portraits have all been recently obtained and are striking counterfeits. The book, which has been enlarged, consists of 196 pages, and is sold at the purely nominal price of threepence.</p>
        <p>LruEaL ME ET ENG AT RUNCORN. On Tuesday night, ate public meeting held under the auspices of the Northwich Division Liberal Associa- tion, which was held in Runcorn Publics Hall, Lord Stanley of Alderley delft at length with the education question. He said no system of national education could be truly efficient and progressive unless it pos- sessed in the widest possible way the principles of public control and public management. We in England had the moral right by Parliamentary action to vest every school which had a trust deed for public educa- tion to vest those schools in the authority charged with the education of the people. (Applause).—Sir John Brunner, who had a magnificent reception, ex- posed Mr. Chamberlain's heresieson the fiscal question, dealing especially with chemicals, glass, and sugar. Mr. Chamberlain, he said was no safe guide for the Empire nor any portion of the Empire.</p>
        <p>We paid £20,000 for the British rights to Liquozone—the highest price ever paid for similar rights on any scientific discovery. We did this after the product had been tested for five years, through physicians and hospitals, in several countries. It had been proved in thousands of the</p>
        <p>most difficult cases obtainable. It had cured every disease which had been considered incurable. We I paid that price because,in germ disea.ses,Liquozone does what medicine cannot do. Now we ask you to try it—try it at our expense. Let us give you a bottle, to prove what it Can do for you. A test is better than .testimonials, better than arguments. Not Medicine.</p>
        <p>Liquozone is not made by compounding drugs nor is there alcohol in it. Its virtues are derived solely from- gas—largely oxygen gas—by a process requiring immense apparatus and 14 days' time. This process has, for more than 20 years, been the constant subject of scientific and chemical re- search.</p>
        <p>The result is a liquid which does much of what oxygen does. It is food for the nerves and the blood—the most helpful thing in the world for you. Its effects are exhilarating, vitalising, purifying. Yet it. is a germicide so certain that we publish on every bottle an offer of £250 for a disease germ that it cannot kill. The reason is that germs are vegetables; and Liquozone—the very life of an animal—is deadly to vegetal matter.</p>
        <p>There lies the great value of Liquozone. It is the only way. known to kill germs in the body without killing the tissues, too. Any drug that kills germs is a poison, and it cannot be taken internally. Medicine is almost helpless in any germ disease. That is why diseases , which have resisted 'medicine for years yield at once to Liquozone. And it 'cures diseases which medicine never cures. In germ- diseases the results are almost inevitable.</p>
        <p>Any physician or hospital not yet using Liquozone will be gladly supplied for a test.</p>
      </div>
