      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Interesting Lecture by filr.T.C. Horsf all</head>
        <p>Interesting Lecture by filr.T.C. Horsf all</p>
        <p>From various standpoints has the question of com- pulsory military service been discussed, but in an in- teresting lecture to the Warrington branch of the National Service League on Tuesday evening, Mr. T. C. Horsfall, the well-known philanthropist, of Manchester, treated the subject in a new light. His contention was that a system of national service would, by creating a spirit of comradeship and mutual interest among all classes, result in a betterment of the condition of the masses. The meeting was held in the Assembly-room at Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons' Works, Bank Quay, Mr. F. W. Monks presiding over a large attendance. The proceedings opened ,vith a selection by the works band which gave an admirable performance of" Gem of Tchaikowsky"—the test piece in the recent competition at the Crystal Palace. In introducing Mr. Horsfall, the chairman said the present was the second of the series of lectures arranged by the committee to promote the objects of the League The subject selected that evening— National service and its relation to the health and welfare of the corn- , munity " was a particularly happy one. They might congratulate themselves on having the opportunity of listening to such an address, and particularly from a gentleman of Mr. Horsfall's experience and ability. He (Mr. Monks) should think there was no one in Manches- ter who had a wider or a longer experience of every- thing which had for its object the improvement of the conditions under which the people lived in our large centres of population than Mr. Horsfall. (Applause.) Mr. HORSFALL, in the course of a long but interesting , paper, said the question of whether it would be an advantage or disadvantage to the councry to have a system of general national service was often discussed in connection with the subject of the best means for maintaining and extending our already vast empire. He had no wish to discuss the question in that light. He had always believed that another question—"ls it desir- able for this country to retain connection with its colonies and dependencies ? " was more important and ought to be answered with" Yes "only by those who were convinced after careful consideration that the connection had a good influence on the condition of the mass of people in this country as well as in our colonies and dependen• cies. He was far from feeling sure that this • country, so long as it continued to neglect the most valuable of all its possessions—the health and strength of its people —was benefitted by the connection. No country could have a very good influence on the conditions of life in I its dependencies or other independent countries, unless it established within its own boundaries conditions which were most conducive to the welfare of all classes of its people. He therefore considered the question of national se/ice chiefly in relation to the welfare of the inhabitants of the United Kingdom. Far less effort had I been and 'was being made here than in Germany and Switzerland to give the mass of the people the power , and will to -reach a high level of physical, mental and I moral life. Comparing the English educational system I with those of the countries he had named, he pointed out that Germany reaped in civil and military life a large harvest of advantage from the desire for advanced education created by national service. We in England had only just begun to make physical training compul- sory in our schools, and we had many badly lighted, ill-ventilated and badly warmed schools in use. It was however, in respect to the environment of the people that our inferiority was most marked. Mr. Horsfall alluded to the building regulations which had to be observed in nearly all large German towns, result- ing in the provision of wide streets and open spaces. Many of the streets were beautifully planted with trees. In no new districts of any German towns were there any slums, or was there the possibility that any slum could exist. After careful search in most large German towns he knew of no district in which workmen who couldafford to pay for sufficient house room and food could not live a full,pleasant and healthy life. He knew of no German workman's house which had not within easy reach a large open space' or tree-planted streets. Nowhere else were there such towns as ours. We stood alone in making</p>
        <p>HEALTH A!.:I'D HAPPINESS ALMOST IMPOSSIBLE in vast urban districts by allowing streets to be so narrow, houses so crowded together, and the air so impure that vegetation could not exist and pleasant exercise could not be taken. One reason for the differ- ence between the conditions here and on the continent was that Governments and the well-to-do classes who had the most influence on the action of Governments, needed to have their good will towards the poorer classes reinforced by a desire to keep those classes fit for military service. In England the majority of the well-to-do classes did not know how miserable was the condition of our town population, and a large proor- tion of the minority who did were not willing, because they had received no training in patriotism, to take the trouble and make the sacrifice which must be incurred by any minority which was to succeed in effecting a social reform.. He contended that the establishment of general military service would do much to improve the state of the towns, the position of working men, the tenure of land, and school system, which were so urgently needed. (Hear, hear and applause.) The annual examination of all our young men who reached the age for service would give to everyone information which no one at present possessed respect- ing the true condition of the people. A new and strong feeling of love towards the country would be created which would make all classes more willing to bear the burdens of service for its welfare when they returned to civil life. General service could not fail to improve THE POSITION OF THE WORKLNGCLASSES as a whole very greatly—to a far greater extent, he thought, than could be reached by any one other change. It would necessarily involve the granting of electoral suffrage to all who passed through the ranks, and that would increase the influence on Parliament of the work- ing classes. At present there were hundreds and thou- sands of workingmen who counted for nothing in either political or social life. They did not seek election to any organized body, they were not members of trades unions, they did not vote for other people and many of them lived under, the most miserable conditions, and would be physically unfitted for service; but some would be fit, and the large number of rejections would compel the general community to improve the existing conditions and to prevent,by good physical training in the schools, by continuation classes, by the feeding of hungry children, and the discouragement of drinking, the creation of any more poor creatures of the kind. Mr. Horsfall contended that in the long run races which were fine physically would not allow themselves to be ruled by those who were poor physically. Yet, we were sending to India a considerable number of English soldiers who were inferior to a large proportion of the Indian soldiers who were taken from the martial races. Had we had a system of general military service.</p>
        <p>NO SUCH CALAMITY AS THE BOER WAR need have occurred. There would have been no Majnba Hill.-The Englishman would have had the consciousness of his well-trained physical power and military efficiency, and of the fact that the oountry could have at any moment placed a large and well trained army in the field. The result would he been that both races would have been enabled to live side by side as self- respecting nations. He believed that certain defeat</p>
        <p>lawaited us if we had to meet on land the forces of any one of the great Powers before we had adopted national service. Mr. Horsfall contended that the argument that , national service would foster love of war was ridiculous and untrue. A knowledge of the dangers and horrors of war would, he throught,tend to create an abhorrence of I it. If the sons of all classes had to serve it would pre- vent unnecessary wars and lead to more generous treat- ment of the relatives of fallen soldiers and the wounded ; If national service were properly explained he had not I the least doubt that the country would soon gladly accept it. He believed if England was to be saved from the grave dangers which now threatened her she ; would adopt the systena. Mr Horsfall paid a tribute Ito the services of Captain Crosfleld on behalf of the League, remarking that he had done more admirable I work for it probably than any man out of London. ' I (Applause). The CHAIRMAN remarked that the more the proposals of the League wereidiscussed, the more the feeling of suspicion was being dissipated. 1 Mr. H. ROBERTS, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Horsfall for his able lecture, said that gentleman Ihad for many years associated himself with everything that made for the moral, social and material improve- ment of the masses of people who lived in big cities like Manchester. (Applause.) As an old municipal administrator he (Mr. Roberts) sympathised with Mr. Horsfall's views upon the conditions under which the vast masses of the working people had to live in the slums of our great towns. We, as municipalities, were MILES BEHIND GERMANY in regard to the power we had of dealing with the con- ditions of life which had been referred to. In many large towns in South Germany, no such thing as slum life existed, and there was no reason why it should exist in England. (Applause.) National service would have to come but if we waited for our members of Parliament or any Government to take up the ques- tion without pressure from the people we should have to wait a long time. Mr. MOSSE seconded. The CHAIRMAN in alluding to the condition of the • working classes, said he would like to prohibit the employment of married women in factories. It was a crying evil, and they ought to agitate at any rate until some restriction was put upon such employment. It would go a long way to improve the health of the com- munity, and be a means of raising much stronger children. (Applause.) The resolution was cairied with enthusiasm. Mr. HORSFALL, in reply, said he detested war, but he had been driven to the conclusion that the logical beginning of all social reform in this country was the introduction of national service. On the motion of the RECTOR, seconded by the Rev. Father Wilson, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman and to the works. Mr. HALL! wELL returned thanks on behalf of the band, who concluded the meeting with an excellent, selection.</p>
      </div>
