      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>4, • 4. _ ".‘"'" • •</head>
        <p>4, • 4. _ ".‘"'" • •</p>
        <p>A run-through in-off into a top pocket, illustrati the combined action of side and the nap of thi cloth. Object white filt inches from the 'WV cushion and 10.2 inches frofn the side cushion. Cue ball 12 inches from the top cushion and 261 fron the side cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 36 shows a run-through into the right top pocket, the cue ball and the object ball being in a line with that part of the side cushion just before the angle The best way to play the in-off in this position is to play quite full on to the object ball with plenty of left hand side, using a medium-pace stroke. Played this way the object ball will strike the cushion at the point marked X. but the left hand side on the cue ball work- ing with the nap of the cloth will pull the cue ball to the left of the path taken by the object ball, and thus instead of striking the top cushion the one ball will go straight into the pocket. In many almost identical positions the in-off might be made by using the other side or no side at all, and hitting the object ball just a little less than full, but in these particular positions the one great thing is to avoid a kiss, and when the object ball is hit a little less than full, a-kiss may easily occur which would ha4re been avoided by a full contact In the position shown on Diagram 36 a kiss can not be prevented emeept by playing dead full on to the object ball. For this reason, thew- fore, whenever the position is very similar to the one shown on Diagram 36 aim should be taken full on to the object ball, and such side should be imparted to the cue ball as will pull it out of the line of aim and towards the pocket. In playing the stroke shown on Diagram 36 into a baulk pocket the reverse side should be used, as aide against the nap has just the reverse effect of side with the nap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>•DIAGRAM</head>
        <p>•DIAGRAM</p>
        <p>A run-through in-off into a centreAocket made by the agency of side and the nap of the cloth. Object white 3} inches from the side cushion and 321 inches from the baulk line, cue ball 511 inches from the side cushion and 21 inches from the baulk line.</p>
        <p>Diagram 37 shows a very pretty stroke into centre pocket played from the baulk end of the table that is, with the nap of the cloth. Played without side, though not exactly impossible, the stroke would be exceedingly difficult as the pocket would be an exceptionally blind 'one. If, however, the object ball be hit quite full with plenty of right hand side and the stroke be played slowly, so as to get the greatest effect from the joi-nt action of side and nap, the cue ball instead of following on in the direction taken by the object ball, will pull to the right and make straight for the pocket, and should it strike the upper angle of the pocket not very far from the fall of the slate, the side which it tarries being pocket side will take it into the pocket. In playing this stroke into a centre pocket from any position .above the centre pocket, the side on the cue ball must be reversed owing to the run-through-being against the nap of the cloth. Played down the table the stroke is not nearly so useful, as the object ball will almost always go into baulk, and if the stroke be played fast enough to bring the ball out again, the cue ball will travel too fast to give the side much chance of- making itself felt. When; 'however, the other object ball happens to be just a little out of baulk, and in a favourable .poSition, the fact that the run-through sends the ball into baulk will not twitter pane4..ae, ow cannon oft the ball which is out of MuM win 'generally be left.</p>
        <p>In playing centre pocket run-throughs similar to the one shown on diagram 37 it will sometimes happen that the position will be such that the object ball will be thrown on to ths farther angle, and when this is the case it will rebound more or iss into the middle of the table, so that from these positions the object ball cannot go into baulk when playing the in-off from above a centre pocket,</p>
        <p>Diagram 33 shows a run-through in-off into a baulk pocket. .A. 5 however, the stroke has to be played against the nap of the cloth, some little allowance must be made for this, as the tendency of the left hand side with which the stroke must be played will be to pull the ball away from the pocket and towards the side cushion. It is best therefore to play the stroke as though the pocket were at the place marked X on the baulk cushion. If the object ball be truly struck and the cue ball immediately after contact is running to- wards X, the side will pull it out of this line and enable it to enter the pocket. It might be thought that as the cue-ball travels across the nap and very 'little against it, the nap would have very little effect on the ball. The nao however, only ceases to affect the path of a</p>
        <p>37 11 • •</p>
      </div>
