      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>ANSWERS TO C RRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS TO C RRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>" Enquirer " (Glasgcw.)—The point 3ou raise is a curious one. May a player in a handicap game, after having potted the red, ask whether previous to the i stroke the red was on the spot or not, n order that he may know whether it will go on the billiard spot or centre spot should he pot it again? There is no rule which covers this point, but unless a 'player is "ex- tremely forgetful he would himself know whether he had potted the red from the spot or not. - "Ivories" (London).—l do not mind your not agree- ing with my remarks of last week re ivory and crystalate balls, but as you admit that you have never played with crystalate balls, you are hardly in a position to compare the balls. It is no proof that because Stevenson's record break of 788 was made with ivory balls.and his best break with cystalate balls is only 703, ivory balls must be the more reliable. You seem to have lost sight of the fact that at the time Stevenson made his break of 703, this was his record, and the fact that he made 788 two or three months afterwards only shows the wonderful improvement in his game. The chief part of my argument, however, was not that crystalate balls were better than the very best ivory though in my opinion they are at least equally as good, but that they were infinitely better than the average ivory balls still to be found in hotels and clubs. This statememt I maintain is incontrovertable. It is impossible for me this week to give a list of Stevenson's principal breaks made with the two balls last season, but I will look these up, and endeavour to give you the particulars next week.</p>
        <p>"Twice off the Spot" (Devizes).—The red goes on the centre spot after being potted twice off the spot, and the red need not necessarily be potted into the top pocket. Even should it be fluked into a baulk pocket it counts as a pot off the spot. Screw (Kidderrainster).—The series of articles on run-throughs will continue for several weeks yet, and will be followed by a chapter on screw. Answers to several correspondents are unavoidably held over till next week.</p>
        <p>RISO LEVI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The Girls in the Pantasaph Orphanage. The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when •there were present':—Mr. Baxter (presiding), the Rev. Father Donlevy, Messrs. C. Linaker, C. E. Linaker. jun., S. Woodward, T. Wright, W.M. Morris, C. Roylance. J. Mounfield,J. Hallows,J. Whitley, J. Douglas, J. Stokes, .T. L. Jones, A. Fryer, J. W. Davies, G. Pilling, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Whitlow, J. B. Chadwick, A. G. Guest, H. Eyes, R. Billington, J. Smith, H. Mawdsley, C.lHorton, R. A. Chrimes, L. Horton, J. Rutter, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk),T. Cooke, J. Riley and W. De Prez (relieving officers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>DESCRIPTION OF THE WORKHOUAE.</head>
        <p>THE DESCRIPTION OF THE WORKHOUAE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Registrar General stating that the alternative description of the Union Workhouse Northwich Road, autton, as suggested by the Board, was not sufficiently definite. On the motion of Mr. Woodward it was decided to describe the workhouse as 50, Northwich Road,Dutton.</p>
        <p>THE GIRLS IN THE PANTASAPH ORPHANAGE. ACTION BY THE BOARD,</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported he had received a letter from Mr. Spruce with regard to the girls in the Pantasaph Orphanage who were chargeable to the Union. He had made inquiries and had ascertained that the four eldest girls from their Board were not physically fit to go out to service when they were between the ages of 15 and 16 years. Mr. Spruce moved "that this Board learns with regret that some of the elder girls in the Pantasaph Orphanage are not weil enough to be put out to service, and that the authorities be informed that this Board considers the elder girls are old enough for service and that as soon as they are physically fit steps will be taken to get them some employment." To his mind, he said, some of the girls were too big to be kept at the expense of the rate- payers. The resolution would do no harm if it was sent to the authorities. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows seconded. He objected to girls being kept in the Orphanage until they were 15 or 16 years of age. Most workingmen's children had to be working before they reached that age. He thought 14 years was plenty old enough. The Rev. Father Donlevy said it would be a difficult matter to say at what age a girl was to go out to work as some were more fit to earn. their own livings at 14 than_others at 16. He could assure the Board that the authorities of the institution were very desirous of sending the girls out to work as soon as they were fit. Mr. Linaker remarked that the extra twelve months' training which was given the girLs in the orphanage was of special benefit, as it enabled them to take up better positions in after life, and he did not think the money which that extra training cost was mis-spent. The Chairman: It is thought, that the children are receiving greater advantages than are enjoyed by the ordinary workingman's children. Mr. Hallows: 1 will propose that the child of every workingman when it has reached the age of 16 years, is sent to the orphanage for twelve months' training. Mr. Woodward said he had nothing to say except in commendation of the administration of the Pantasaph Orphanage. At the same time he did not think the passing of the resolution would do any harm. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF A CHAPLAIN</head>
        <p>THE APPOINTMENT OF A CHAPLAIN</p>
        <p>On the -M-otisji; of Mr. Woodward, Messrs. Stokes, Spruce, Linaker, Chadwick, J. Mounfield, Guest, Wood- ward, Baxter, and Norman were appointed a committee to consider the question of appointing a chaplain to the Workhouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>REQUEST BY .THE LADIES.</head>
        <p>A REQUEST BY .THE LADIES.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Visiting Committee requested that several of their members might be allowed to pay occasionyl visits to the Workhouse without giving notice of theic intention to do so.</p>
        <p>The request being contrary to the rules of the com- mittee it was decided to write declining to grant it.</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of failures in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, December 9th, was 171, viz.:—New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 96; deeds of arrangement registered, 75. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: bank- ruptcies, 100 ; deeds of arrangement, 87—total IS7 ; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 269,and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in1904,t0 date, of 440.</p>
