        <p>The LIST of APPLICA,TIONS for DIIBENTURES and PREFERENCE SHARES in WOOLCOMBERS. LIMITED, will CLOSE for both TOWN and COUNTRY on TUESDAY NEXT, the 27th day of December, 1904,</p>
        <p>WOOLCOAIBERS, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>(Incorporated under the Companies Acts, 1862 to 19C0.)</p>
        <p>The Prospectus of the above-named Company, Dated Bradford, 15th December, 1904, and which has been filed with the Registrar of Joint Stock Companies, is being leaned, and the following information is extracted from the document in question.</p>
        <p>CAPITAL:</p>
        <p>250,000 7 per Cant. Cumulative Preference Shares of .C 1 each.—.— ....... ...... .C 250,000 50,000 Ordinary Shares of £1 each 50,000</p>
        <p>4-.1, per Cent. First Itortgage Debenture ....... ...--.—.—... £325,000</p>
        <p>Tllll DBERNTURE STOOK will be issued in multiples of £l, and is intended to be secured by a Trust Deed constituting a specific First Mortgage upon the Freehold and Leasehold Pr operties now purchased by the Company (other than pro- perties held on yearly or shorter tenancies), and by afl )ating first charge upon all the other assets of the Company. The Stock will be redeemable at the option of the Company, on or after the 30th June, 1919, at par, or before that date, at a premium of 5 percent.. subject to six months' notice in either case, The Stock will be repayable at the same premium if the principal becomes payable before that date by reason of a winding-up or default in payment of interest. The Company reserves power to substitute other property or cash in lieu of, and of a value equal to, any property to be specifically mortgaged to which a good holding title may not be made by the Company. Interest will be paid half-yearly on the Ist Sannar y and lit of July in each year. The first payment will be made on Isl July neat, calculated as from the dates of payment of the amounts due on application and allotment.</p>
        <p>THE PREFERENCE SHA.RES are entitled to a cumulative dividend at the rate of 7 per cent. per annum, and rank in priority to the Ordinary Bhare3 in the case of any diitribution of the assets of the Company. .</p>
        <p>THERE ARE BEING OFFERED FOR SUBSCRIPTION AT PAR-</p>
        <p>210,000 7 PER CENT. PREFERENCE SHARES OF Li EH, AND</p>
        <p>£325,000 41 PER CENT. FIRST MORTGAGE DEBENTURE STOCK</p>
        <p>Payable as follows : Debenture Stock. Preference Shares. On Application On Allotment 5O per cent. And the balance due on the Preference Shares in calls of not more than Si. each at intervals of not less than wo months.</p>
        <p>Preference in allotment will be given to holders of Shaves and Debenture Stock in the Yorkshire Woo!cambers ssociation (Limited): in proportion to sir h):11o43 Sin.it also to the unsecured trade creditors of the Association.</p>
        <p>Any of the above Debenture Stock not subscribed for on this issue will be taken by the Debenture Stock-holders of the Yorkshire Woolcombers' Association (Limited) thcough their Reseiver, and any of the Preference Shares now offered not subscribed for will be taken by the Underwriters.</p>
        <p>The TRUSTEES FOR THE DEBENTURE STOOK HOLDERS are teIiORGE CHESTER HAWORTH, 35, Dale-street, Manchester. JAMES HEALD, Ellerslie, Didsbury, near Manchester.</p>
        <p>The DIRBOTORS are</p>
        <p>JOHN H. LACY BALDWIN (Messrs. J. and 3. Baldwin and Partners, Limited, Halifax), Broomfield, Halifax, Worsted Spinner. JOHN ALBERT BRIGHT (Messrs. John Bright and Brothers, Limited, Rochdale), One Ash, Rochdale, Cotton Spinner. WILLIAM DALE SHAW (Messrs. Shaw and Shaw, Huddersfield), Hoimeileid. Longwood. near Huddersfield, Cotton Spinner. JOSEPH WHEATLEY (Messrs. Henry Wheatley-land Sons, Mirlield,) Woodlands, Mirfield, Woollen Manufacturer.</p>
        <p>The BANKERS are LONDON CITY AND MIDLAND BANKf(LIY ITED), Head Oill3e, Threadneedle-street, London, E.Ol and Branches. HALIFAX AND HIIDDEBSFIBLD UNION BANKING COMPANY (LIMITED), Head Office, Halifax, and Branches,</p>
        <p>The SOLICITORS are WADB, BILBROUGH, TBTLBY and CO., Bradford. For the Company— { HAROLD MARSHALL, Halifax. For the Trustees—BOOTß, BDGAR.Iand CO., 20, Both-street, Manchester. For the Contractors—BURN and BBRRIDGB, 11, Old Br:ad-street, London.</p>
        <p>The BROKBIIS are London —{ COATES, SON, and CO., 99, Gresham-street, 8.C., and Sto3k Hicohange. GOVBTT, SONS. and CU., 6, Throgmortou-street. 8.C., and Stock likchsage, idanehester—J. LOICASHIRB andloo., 46, Pall-Mall. Bradford— JOHN GURNBY and SONS, 29, Kirkg‘te. Huddersfield —F. W. BONTLBY and CO., Estate-buildings. Leeds—F. W. BBNTLEY and CO., Yorkshire Post-chambers.</p>
        <p>The AUDITORS are ARISIITAGS and NORTON, Chsrtered Acoonntants, Station-street-buildings, Utilderfil, and Brsdford. THOMAS PATON and CO., Chartered ACCOUtltants, Cheapside, Bradford,</p>
        <p>The 811CRBTABY (pro tem.) is</p>
        <p>3. H. ROSCOE.</p>
        <p>The BEGIdTERED OFFICE is at 45, Well-street, Bradford.</p>
        <p>The LONDON OFFICE (pro tem.) Is at 17, Old Broad-street, London, B.C.</p>
        <p>Woolcombers, Limited is formed to acquire. as on and from the 31st Decembar, 19)4. the (min undertaking and goodwill of the Yorkshire WoolCombers' Association, Limited, hereinafter c tiled the Association (except b3ok and othe.r trade debts, bills and notes receiveable, investments representing debts already (inflected, cash and certain rights of action referred to in the Prospeatus),and to carry on the business with the benefit of the restrictive covenants obtaine d by the Association from its Vendors as noW in forge, as well as of certain new and advantageous arrangemer te later on referred to. --- - -</p>
        <p>The Association Was formed in 1899 for the purpose of amalgamating the woolcombing businesses of 38 Companies and firms. some of which have been disposed of or otherwise dealt with, and carries on a large business. Owing to over capitalisation. and adverse trade conditions, mainly consequent upon the drought in Australia, and the Association having been also embarrassed by a portion of its working capital having been devoted to the acquisition of Patents and of other Mills not Included in the original amalgamation, the result was that iniNovember, 1932, a Receiver and Manager was appointed on behalf of the Debenture Stockholders, and such Receiver had since carried on the business. . . _</p>
        <p>Under an Agreement intended to to entered into with the Company and the Trustees for its Debelture Stock- holders by Messrs. Henry Ayrton, James Will. Isaac Smith, and Francis Willey, the principal Top Makers connected with the Association, thsy will jointly and a v erally guarantee that for ten years from the 31st December, 1904, the profits derived from the Company's business suall be at least sufficient tol pay the interest ;on the present issue of Debentnre Stock.</p>
        <p>Under Agreements already entered into with the Company each of them the above-named Top Makers also guarantees that subject to the usual strike, fire, and act of God clauses, the profits of the Company's branch, formerly owned by his respective firm or Company, shall amount during such ten years after providinc for depreciation as therein stated to the yearly sum mentioned in such Agreements, the aggregate of the sums respectively gusranteed being £30,350 annually.</p>
        <p>The Agreements last referred to further provide that* Ia cmsileratian of the ablva guarantees, the guarantors will In each year in which the net profits, after providing for depreciation, at their respective branches or works exceed the sums guaranteed by them respectively receive one-half of the excess profits so earned. In the event of the woolcombing required by any guarantor being beyond the capacity of the branch or works with which he is connected, such guarantor. on giving a guarantee against loss, may use an additional branch or branches, receiving one-half of the profits of each additional branch or branches.</p>
        <p>These arrangements were entered Into with a view to securing the co-operation of the Top Makers, and the sending Of their wool to the new Company.</p>
        <p>The following figures show the great reduction effected in the cipitaligation of the new Company : CAPITAL, Former Company. Present Company. Debenture Stock , £760,066 Preference Shares 661,074 Ordinary Shares 544,631 --</p>
        <p>21,965,821</p>
        <p>2625,000</p>
        <p>It Is believed that,the profits of the Association have been largely affected by the ceases mentioned in the Prospectus Notwithstanding the. adverse conditions, the following certificates of profits, prepared at the instance of the contractors has been given by Messrs. Bdwia Guthrie and Co., of Manchester, and Messrs. Thomss Paton and C 3.. of Bradfor d Chartered Accountants, as follows : 1 To the Directors of WOOLCOMBBRS, LI MITBD. beg to report that we have examined the books and accounts of the Yorkshire Woolcombers' Gentlemeo,—We Association, Limited, for the five years ending 30th June, 1904, and we certify that the profits for that period, after charging administration expenses, but before providing for Debenture, Loan, or other interest, Bxecutive Directors' remuneration, Income-tax, or:depreciation,' have been as under :</p>
        <p>£ s. d. For the Year ending) June c3Oth, 1900 (a) ... ... ... ... 92.024 18 6 .. „ 1901 ... ... ... ... ... 29,719 3 4 Showing an annual if ” 1902 —..— .—.— —. 62,445 410 average of PP ' PP 1903 ... ... ... ... .- 62,468 16 E £51,813 13s. sd. 1904 (b) ... •.. ... ... 22,410 4 3 Pi PP In arriving at the profits for the period subsequent to 25th November, 1932, since which date the business Was been carried on by a Receiver and Manager on behalf of the Debenture Stoccholders, no charge has been male in respect of his costs and remuneration. (Signed) EDWIN GUTHRIB &amp; CO.. Chartered Accountants, 71, King-street, Manchester. (Signed) THOMAS PATON &amp; CO., Chartered Accountants, 23, Cbeapsids, Bradford. BRADFORD, 2nd December, 1904.</p>
        <p>(a) This amount includes profits earned by Vendors prior to the incorporation of the Company. and which were th a dealt with in accordance with the Articles of Association. (b) During this period and from the 25th November, 1932, the business was in the Receiver's hands. The respective amounts required annually to pay Debenture Interest and Preference Dividend in the past and under the present issue are as follows IN THII PAST— 2 IN THE PUTUAR— 2 4 per cent. on £760,066 Debenture Stock 30,4021 41 per cent. on £325,003 Debenture Stock £561,074 Preference . .. 33,053 7 „ I £2lO 000 Preference —1 -- £63,4551 £29,325</p>
        <p>On four only of the businesses or branches to be taken over the minimum profits (after deducting depreciation) have been guaranteed as above stated for a period of 10 years at sums aggregating £30,350 per.annum. This amount, subject to Income-tax, may be expected to be increased, as this Company will receive half of the profits earned at the respective Branches of the Guarantors beyond the said guaranteed sums. Negotiations have been opened for further guarantees in respect of some of the other branches.</p>
        <p>The Company also acquires the British rights of Morel's Patent fo. removiag burrs or vegetable matter, which rights are stated by the Secretary of the Association to have cost some £3B 070.</p>
        <p>The present issue provides a sum of £lOO,OOO cash, which, after meeting such expenses as are not payable by the Contractors as hereinafter mentioned, wilt be available as working capital, arid it is norstidered this w.l: be sufficient to pay the Receiver and Manager for the raw material and consumable stores (to be taken over by the Company at cost ptice) and for the work in progress (to be taken over at a valuation as on the 31st December. 1904), and to provide als) for the ordinary purposes of the Company.</p>
        <p>The 40,000 unissued Preference Shares will be available for the provision of farther working capital if required.</p>
        <p>The new Company takes over the property entirely free from the litigation which mole in connection with the promotion of the old ASIOCiatIOII</p>
        <p>The Goodwill is comprised in the purchase, but no portion of the purchase consideration is specifically paid or ap- propriated for it.</p>
        <p>The preliminary expenses, which are payable by the Contractors, are estimated at £12,00.</p>
        <p>_ The minimum subscription oia which the Directors will commence Allotment is ht f the Preference Shares now offered.</p>
        <p>Full Prospectuses and Forms of Application may be obtained from the Bankers. Brokers, Auditors, or Solicitors, or at the Offices of the Company, and will be forwarded on receipt of telegrams addressed " Woolcomber, Bradford," or "Sardegna. London."</p>
        <p>This notice is not issued for the purpose of obtaining subscriptions, but for information only. and no applications will be entertained unless the same are made on the footing of and with reference to the full Prospectus.</p>
      </div>
