      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 17,1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 17,1904.</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR. A Christmas Stocking for Poor Children. Sir,—Oa Christmas Eve between the hours o 10 p.m. and 1 a.m. I hope, assisted by a band of workers to visit at least 600 homes (2.000 children) of the deserving poor, and propose to leave for each child a Christmas stocking. If any reader of your paper is desirous of introducing a little happiness into the life of some poor child he could not find a better means of , doing so than by assisting the work here suggested. The homes will be visited irrespective of political or religious opinions and when I state that there are not more than six families in attendance at my own church eligible for the list you will see that my action is purely a disinterested one. Gifts of oranges, apples, mince-pies, sweets, toys of all descriptions, stockings, boots and shoes may be sent to the Golborne street schoolroom any evening next week, but if possible not later than Thursday or Friday 7 p.m. Gifts of money will be thankfully re- ceived by myself or Mr. T. Longshaw 3S, Bewsey Road and acknowledged by him.—Yours truly HERBERT DUNNICO. 24, Fairfield Road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The Workhouse Extension. Sir,—There seems to be a general impression in the town that the Guardians are rushing headlong into a vast and unnecessary expense without any regard to the interests of the rate,pavers. On the contrary, the Workhouse Enlargement Scheme has only been evolved after very careful and lengthy deliberation by the most experienced men on the Board. The House is already full to overflowing and it is difficult to know haw we are to get through the present winter. If we do not take steps in the matter tbe Local Government Board will compel us to do so, and further delay will mean further expense. This was what happened in the case of our Workhouse Infirmary. A good deal has been said about the desirability of helping families in times of distress and want of w,,rk, and thus preventing them from coming permanently on the rates. Unfortunately the Guardians are only able to fulfil the functions that the law allows ; they relieve actual destitution, but are not allowed to give money to able-bodied men. This is the function of private' charity which in many towns is wisely administered by means of the Charity Organization Society. Those who work amongst the poor know the great evils of a lax system of outdoor relief. It is only too easy to destroy the self-respect and independ- ence of those who receive it. and if we continue to increase it, and are not in a position to apply the indoor test,pauperism already rife amongst us, will become a crushing burden to the town and the rates will increase to a quite unbearable extent.—Your truly. CONSTANCE H. BROADBENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Continued from, page 5.</p>
        <p>Music Licences for Public-Houses.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Warrington Police Court yesterday granted fourteen-day music licences to the following applicants :—T. Walsh,Crown and Sceptre; J. C. Rigby, Queen's Hotel; George Lang, Rose and Crown; R. Perdue,Higher Angel; T. B. Potter,King's Arms; and Mrs. Anderton, Cross Keys. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt. New Curates for the Parish Church. Tha Rev. T. W. Barber has been appointed to a curacy at the Parish Church in succession to the Rev. A. S. Cameron, who has accepted the living of Weston, Lincolnshire. Mr. Barber was ordained by the late Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Ryle) in 1891, and his first curacy was at Holy Trinity Church, Warrington, and his second at Longsight. He was appointed senior curate of St. Paul's, Warrington, in July, 1903. He will have charge of the St Clement's Mission.—The Rev. G. A. Dearden, 8.A., of St. John's College, Cambridge, will succeed the Rev. G. W. Dickinson. Mr. Dearden is at present at St. Benedict's, Ardwick, Manchester. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING Tosros.-63, SANKEY' STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Peace Sunday. Peace Sunday, by common agreement, is the " Sunday next before Christmas." This will fall on the 18th inst. The usual invitation has been sent by the Committee of the Peace Society to all ministers in charge throughout the country, to some Sunday school teachers and others, and to the Protestant pastors in France, making a total of 33,000. Offer is also made, as usual, of a selection of peace literature, which will be supplied gratis. for personal use, on application to the Secretary, Dr. W. Evans Darby, 47, New Broad- street, London, E.C. A large number of acceptances and applications for literature has been received. The Secretary will be glad to hear from any who may hink of keeping the day. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING or TONICS. I 63. SA.NKEV-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Workingmen's Mission, Thewlis-street. • The first Band of Hope entertainment of the season was given in the schoolroom last Monday evening before a large audience, over which Mr. W. H. Sherburn presided. The programme included a piano- forte duet by Mr. Atkinson and Miss Oakley; songs by Misses E. Dunne and A. Oakley; recitations by Misses Kinsey, C. Whitby,ll. Gilbert, A. Lawton and Masters A. Dennett, A. Hunt, J. Barratt, arid T. Whitby. Two violin solos were rendered by Master James Watts, accompanied by Miss Watts. Miss B. Leigh also contributed a pianoforte solo. The programme concluded with a dialogue entitled Matthew Brown's Vow," which was very successfully performed. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLA.Sb MINERAL WATERS.—Adn. Good Templars' Meeting. The monthly meeting of the 1.0.G.T. No. 1 Con- vention, South-West Lancashire was held on Saturday, in the Good Templar Hall, Railway-road, Leigh. Bro. J. Parr, D.C2T. (Leigh), presided over a fair attend • ance, 'Warrington being represented by Bro. C. J. Sheath, C.T., and Sister A. Littlewood,, S.J.T. The report of the Secretary, Bro. E. Newman (Earles- town) showed improved work among the lodges and temples throughout the area, Warrington coming in for a fair share of praise. There was algreat improve- ment in the programmes; the lodges having introduced more educational items. Hopes were entertained that several new lodges would be opened in the near future at Earlestown, Leigh, G-olborne and bee. An encouraging address was given by Bro. G. 13. Walker District Treasurer (Liverpool). UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Band of Hope Concert. The Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Band of Hope held their first concert of the season in the schoolroom on Monday evening before a large audience. The follow- ing programme was submitted :—Recitations by Hilda Seed, May Payne, Harold Carr, Fred Hatfield, Fred Payne, and Mr. W. Wilkes; songs by Miss L. Plinston, and Messrs. B. Jennings and E. Jones ; violincello solos by Mr. John Jackson. The conclud- ing item was a humorous dialogue entitled" Four after one," the following taking part : —The Misses F. Moore and R. Cartledge and Messrs. E. Jones, W. Wilkes and P. Jackson. Miss Alice Woodland and Mr. C. Powney, who had promised to contribute to the programme, were prevented from attending by indisposition. Councillor T. Barton presided and gave an encouraging address. The accompanist was Mr. E. G. Rickers.—On the motion of Mr. S. Blythe, seconded by Mr. Corradine, a vote of thanks was passed to all who had taken part in the proceedings. The singing of the Doxology brought a most success- ful gathering to a close. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. School Attendance Officers' National Association. The annual meeting of the Warrington sad district branch of, the above Ass .)oiation was held in the Education Office, Sankey-street, Warrington, on Saturday last, Mr. W. Bamber in the chair. The secretary and tileasurer (Mr. W. Rutter), read his annual report from which it appeared that four new members had joined the branch during the year: making a total membership of 23. The report and balance sheet were passed, and the following officials were appointed for the ensuing year, viz. :—President, Mr. Thomas Martindale (Earlesta ma) ; vice-president, Mr. Thomas Bromilow (St. Helens) ; secretary and treasurer, gr. William Rutter (Warrington).; com- mittee :—Nlessrs. W. B imber, J. H. Searisbrick and W. Farrar (Warrington), W. Mercer (St. Helens), and W. Panuill (G-olborne). It was resolved that the next quarterly meeting be held at Warrington on Saturday February 4th,1905. Votes of thanks to the retiring officials and the chairman concluded the business;</p>
        <p>XMAS PRESENTS.-Try J. S. GARIAND',S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street,Warrington.Splendid n.—Advt. Warrington's Covered Cars. WarriVon can now boast the possession of a highly desirable acquisition in the shape of a cov3red car, and it is hoped that during the course of the next week or two, three more of these improved cars will be placed at the disposal of the numerous patrons of the service. The appearance of a covered car on the Warrington system excited no little attention and its progress on the trial trip on Monday was watched with considerable interest. It is of the usual type and th covering affords ample protection against rain and generally inclement weather. The ventila- tion of the added portion is under the con- trol of the conductor who operates the sliding sides by means of a key placed at each end of the` car. The seating accommodation of the top has been increased from 33 to 33, the increase being obtained. without the passengers being in any way inconveni- enced. In the lower part' of the car a two-faced clock has been hung from the centre of the rogf and doubt less this will prove a great boon to the travellers. The car has been running throughout the week, a different route being selected each day, and so great is the improvement upon the ordinary car that one is tempted to wonder why the departure was not taken earlier.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT: UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN L ANCASHUM—Advt. Warrington Spiritualist Society. Mr. Stout spoke strongly in defence of spiritualism at the weekly meeting of the Warrington Slciety, which was held in the Druid's Hall on Sunday. Those who stated that spiritualism was a fraud ana a`thing to be shunned were not justified in doing so unless they had studied and investigated the subject for themselves and had arrived at that conclusion from their own experience, and not from hearsay. To declare that the communicating intelligences were mostly if not altogether evil spirits dis- played ignorance of the laws governing the return of spirits, and was a gross misconcep- tion of either the power or the will of God. And as in the old dm s, so t3-day, spiritual manifesta- tions occurring outside the control of the priests and parsons were set down as emanating ff ma the devil. The reason was not far to seek. It was against the interests of the cloth. Returning spirits taught a, religious of deeds instead of creeds, and led Spiritual- ists to care naught for clerics (as such) or clericalism, and to accept no teaching which di!l not accord with their reason and present-day experience. On Monday evening a social was held, the programme being pro- vided by members of the Lyceum. I number of instrumental duets and solos were given by, Messrs. L. Edwards and S. Dark, accompanied on the piano by Mr. Fred. Foster, and vocal quartets were given by Messrs. W. Massey, Bert, Fred and Arthur Foster. The programme also included a few phonograph selections and games.: .•</p>
      </div>
