      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>UNEMPLOYED PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Discussed by the Social Service League The problem of the unemployed .was under discussion at the weekly debate in connection with the Social Service League last evening. The members of the Cairo-street Literary and Debating Society attended, and the Rev. C. Harvey Cook opened the debate with an interesting paper. Dealing with society as an organism and not as an organization,the rev. gentleman asked his hearers to bear in mind that no member of society could fulfil his own progress apart from that of the rest. They were not isolated atoms, floating in sun- beams, separated from all the rest. They were parts of a whole. As Paul asserted of the body physiCal that "when one member suffers all the members suffered with it," so students of the body social said that when one member suffered, al/ the members suffered with it. The pathos and tragedy were that their social sensibilities were so largely numb. The solution of the various social problems must come from spiritual sympathies and ethical emotions and not on the basis of pounds. shillings, and pence. He pro- ceeded to divide the subjects into two heads, the necessity for immediate action, and the necessity for preventative action. Considering the first question, he sug- gested the establishment of labour bureaux and also that the local authorities should have power to employ men to cleanse and limewash back yards and outhouses, and so forth, in the interests of public health. The rates need not be affected by these measures, as the cost should fall on the landlords and property owners. As showing the reasonableness of this suggestion, he instanced that railway companies had to limewash their cattle trucks every time they used them. Other relief measures advocated were the cultivation of forestry and the reclamation of waste lands. It was wiser, Mr. Cook I contended, for the municipality to pay a larger wages bill than a larger Workhouse bill, because an increased wages bill would represent "something attempted, something done," whereas the Workhouse bill was only something spent. He suggested borough betterment rather than poor law relief. Great numbers of the unemployed might be engaged in the construc- tion of new streets, resetting streets, sewage works. flagging entries, repairing bridges, and the erection of public buildings, such as proper slaughter-houses and markets. As to the necessity of preventative measures, what was needed was the teaching of the science of living. Had this been understood fifty years ago we should have had no problem of the unemployed. He supported Ruskin's notion of Government workshops for the education in manual exerises of all the boys of the country. Mr. Cook's idea of dealing with the present situation was not" back to the land" or emigration, for the trend of things was towards the city. Therefore powers should be acquired by the municipalities for the extension of cities on a scientific basis, with compulsory purchase of land. Relief works were no solution of the problem, but only a palliative. All unemployed labour was economic loss. Manufacturers said that machinery lying idle was worse than machinery running at a loss. So the vast human power latent in the unemployed was not only a dissipated force but an economic loss. At the same time nothing should be done that would harmfully affect the delicate machine called private enterprise. Tile drevuer must go before the practical man to be a s 3rt of John the Baptist to open up the way for a time when a man willing to work and unable to find work should no longer represent "fortune's saddest sight under the sun." A somewhat lengthy discussion ensued in which the general opinion was that something should be done as soon as possible to deal with the questton. The inter- ference of tne State was advocated, and several of Mr. Cook's suggestions were approved. The following resolution was passed:—That this meeting of the Social Service League is of the opinion that the request of the Tramway servants for a holiday on Christmas Day is a reasonable one, and respectfully urges the Tramways Committee to accede to same, without reduction of wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>Gift by Mr. Linnaeus Greening. The Warrington municipal museum finds itself, not for the first or second time, the recipient of a handsome gift from Mr. Linnwus Greening. It will be remem- bered that, some years ago, Mr. Greening presented to the museum a fine collection of reptiles and batrachia, preserved in spirit, and a smaller series of spiders, also in spirit, an mounted in an attractive and ingenious manner by he donor's own hands. These collections, like all natural history specimens preserved in liquid, cannot be exposed continuously to the daylight, except at the cost of losing their colour, but the reptile room in which they are placed, is accessible on application to anyone who wishes to see the collections. since the date of the former gift Mr. Greening has continued the study of the same groups of animals, and the present donation includes the specimensacquired by him in the interval, together with a number of others specified below. The most important section of the gift, to students at any rate, is the collection of spiders. These are arranged in more than 1,100 tubes, named and fully labelled with all necessary data, the number of speci- mens being estimated at about 3,000. This accession will greatly add to the prestige of the Museum as a place tor the study of this group of creatutes. The collection of reptiles and batrachia' referred to as already in the Museum, is increased by about 60 specimens, representing nearly as many additional species, and there are in addition some valuable illustra- tions of the embryology of these and other vertebrates. IA box which will interest students contains a series, of 154 specimens,of the Yellow UnderwingMoth,Tryphaena pronnha, being a single brood all reared under identical conditions, and showing practically infinite variety of marking and colouration yet without passing the bounds of "specific " characters. A series of 60 slides of insects, parts of insects and other objects mounted between plates of glass are specially interesting as experiments in the preparation ' of specimens for class or lecture purposes. A personal interest in addition to their undoubted scientific value, attaches to two glass cases containing respectively a series of British wood-boring insects with fragments of timber perforated by eachspecies, and a pair of wrvnecks. These two groups were mounted by the late Mr. Noah Greening,who is well remembered as an enthusiastic entomologist and an expert amateur taxidermist. The specimens throughout are in a perfect state of preservation,. and along with them have come the cabinets and cases in which they are arranged, and a great store of glass jars and other valuable preserving material which represents a considerable value in money. Mr. Greening having determined to discon- tinue for the present the active colleetion of natural history specimens, it illustrates his public spirit and generosity that he should have determined to place his accumulated treasures at once at the service of the public. That his interest in natural scienoe has come to an end no one who knows him will imagine for a moment. A portion of the gift will be on view for a few days in the Bird-room, but the fading which results from exnosure to light makes it necessary to limit the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
