      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Property Owners' Protest.</head>
        <p>The Property Owners' Protest.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley evidently intends to maintain the attitude he has taken up with reference to the Work- house extension scheme, and at Wednesday's meeting of the Board of Guardians he passed further severe strictures upon the opposition of the Property Owners' Association. The Clerk (Mr. J. C. Sutton) submitted a letter from Mr. T. J. Hopkins, secretary of the Associa- tion, stating that he had been requested to forward copy of a resolution passed at a public meeting of property owners and ratepayers held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on the sth inst., and he earnestly hoped the Clerk would present the same to the Board at their next meeting. The resolution read as follows That this meeting of property owners and ratepayers offers a respectful protest against any further expendi- ture on new buildings in connection with the Warrington Workhouse. Considering the present depressed state of trade, they are of opinion that it is an inopportune time for any such expenditure and trust that the Board of Guardians will reconsider their decision to erect new buildings. Mr. FORSHAW said he was present at the protest meeting and he would now move that the letter be suit- ablyand courteously acknowledged. He hoped it would notinfluence the Guardians in any way in their action with reference to the building scheme. The public meeting was called to protest against the extension,but the 'chairman said its primary object was to obtain members for the Property Owners' Association. The CHAIRMAN (Mr. A. Brooks) : May I suggest that as we shall not be able to discuss the matter this morning, you might confine yourself to the resolution acknowledging the letter. All these things will have to be discussed later. Mr. JOLLY: They have been discussed. The CHAIRMAN : They have been discussed and will have to be. If you confine yourself to the resolution, it will tend to the harmony of the meeting. (Hear, hear.) Mr. FORSHA.W : I don't care for too much harmony. (Laughter.) The speaker was understood to add that at the public meeting one Guardian made a speech in which he vilified the Board. -Mr. HICKMAN: I shall ask for that statement to be withdrawn. I vilified no one. The CHAIRMAN: I did not hear the statement. Mr. 'HICKMAN: He charges one of the members of the Board with getting up on a public platform and vilifying the Guardians. I ask for that statement to be withdrawn. . . . -</p>
        <p>The CAA.IRM : If that was the statement Made, I did not hear it. ft is not a proper statement aid I think Mr. Forshaw will be the first to see that and to withdraw it. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSITANW: It is the truth. Mr. HICKMAN: It is not the truth. Mr. JoLLF,Y : I second the motion. The letter is only part of the disgraceiul and discreditable agitation got up and engineered by methods which can only earn the scorn and contempt of every honest man. The Rev. Dr. MERCER said he would like the Board to thank the Property Owners' Association for their resolution and to inform them that the matter had already been fully discussed, but it would have to be discussed again shortly and the Board would give the resolution the utmost possible consideration. The CHAIRMAN said it was understood that a courteous reply should be sent. The CLERK: My answer will be acknowledging the receipt of the letter, and saying that the Board will in due time give it consideration. The CHAIRMAN : It is not a matter of what they will do, but of what they will have to do. The whole thin.' is to be brought up. The proposition to suitably acknowledge the letter was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. WEEK ENDING DECEMBER 10th, 1904. During the week, the following births and deaths were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 44, deaths, 21. The excess of births over deaths was 23. The birth-rate was equal to 33.5. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 15.9 per 1,000 against 15.9 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 178 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 2.2 per 1,000, against 1•6 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 3•8 the corresponding period of 1903. The ages of mortality were :- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15—g5 25-65 65—up. Total 551—' 6 4 21 The deaths included-2 from scarlet fever, 1 from influenza, 1 from diarrhcea, 1 from phthisis, 1 from other tubercular diseases, 3 from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease, 1 from accidents, 10 from all other causes.</p>
        <p>A striking and most delightful display of useful and reliable novelties for Christmas gifts may be seen at Messrs. Beresford's of 77, Church-street Liverpool. Anyone wanting gifts at reasonable prices would do well to see the superb variety in their two windows. In the one purses, dressing-bags. card, letter, cigar and cigarette cases and handsomely fitted dressing-cases are to be seen in so delightful a profusion that it would make one hesitate over a choice ia so excellent a variety. The other window is most tastefully filled with ivory, silver, ebony, and satinwood toilet-brushes. They are making a speciality of ladies' brushes in handsome plush cases. These make a most delightful present, while the gentlemen's cases of military brushes are equally service- able. Altogether one would do well to visit Messrs. Beresford's at 77, Church-street. Liverpool before finally deciding on Christmas and New Year gifts. Their motto seems to be reasonable prices for reliable quality.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. There is nothing to congratulate Wzirrington u pon in regard to their latest performance. They took a strong team to Salford, but they never showed any superiority over the Reds, who succeeded in notching' a winning point five minutes from the close. Warring- ton had numerous chances to score, but they dis- played little ability to grasp their opportunities. Both on paper and on results they were a mach better side thanSalford, but no one would have imagined them to'be the third club in the League on Saturday's form. Salford were, no better certainly, but they gained the day through a fine individual effort on the part of Lomas, just when everybody had resigned themselves more or less contentedly to a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>Both the weather and the ground were against good football, but I have seen vastly better exhibitions under infinitely worse conditions. This was the first occasion of the season when the teams had a wet and heavy ball to play with, and probably the change had something to do with the poor combination shown. Rain had fallen heavily all morning, and though the weather was fine throughout the match the ground was soft and heavy for running. Warrington were short of Dickenson but under the circumstances Brooks did quite as well as could have been expected.</p>
        <p>Salford's experiment of placing Lomas on the wing opposite Fish was one which aroused considerable interest, and though the Salford crack undoubtedly outplayed Fish all the afternoon I consider the experiment a failure. With Lomas either at half or centre, the victory of the Reds must have been more pronounced. Until the occasion when he scored, the burly Cumbrian had caused little anxiety, for the simple reason that he got few opportunities. A man of his calibre in a team like Salford is wasted on the wing. Sometimes the same thing applies to Fish—on 1 one of those days when the centres are" off" and he gets little to do. In all probability, however, the Salfordiaus were well satisfied. They saw Fish, who did such damage in the same match a year ago, com- pletely bottled up, and they also saw Lomls win the game.</p>
        <p>Speaking of Fish—well it is not a pleasant occupation on this occasion. He played about the worst game.l have seen him play anywhere. He was neither in condition nor in form, and anybody watching the renowned left-winger for the first time at Weaste must have considered him a much over-rated player. To see Fish drop a pass is a novelty, and he dropped several on Saturday while his general field work was remarkably ineffective. For once in a way he was practically useless, and but for Lomas's generosity in the way of tackling he must have been severely mauled. Either Fish was not fit to play or he was not trying. Anyhow he injured his reputation con- siderably and helped Warrington to lose the match.</p>
        <p>The game itself was far from interesting. Play was generally scrappy, neither set of backs showing any pretension to classy football. The forwards on both sides played a rattling good game. Hallam was the best man on the Warrington team and did sterling work, while D. Preston at half-back for Salford was exceedingly smart and nippy. He repeatedly got away from the Warrington halves, and opened out the game for the try which Lomas obtained. By the way, the wing man ought to have been stopped long before he reached the line, but 'tams ever so! He slipped Fish at the outset, and that did the trick.</p>
        <p>Salford's back division is composed of a good deal of raw material, though it compared well enough with Warrington's more experienced band in such a game as the one at Weaste. The Warrington three- quarters are a lot below par. Even Harris is not show- ing his usual pace and dash, while the combination is lamentable. Halves and centres will both have to makes more headway or there will need to be some changes. My own opinion is that J. Davies is the man to get the threequarters going, and if he returns from the land of the leek he ought to be well tried with the seniors. He seems to me to possess the enterprise and individuality which are sadly required.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows :- -For- -Against- P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pt. Bradford 15...10... 3... 2...17 26-112...14 17- 79...22 Oldham 14...10... 4... 0...20 22-106-.16 10- 62...20 Wigan l5. 9. 5. 1..16 19- 89...20 12- 76...19 Hunslet - 68...18 Waregen 14... 8... 5... 1...18 21- 99...11 12- 58...17 Leeds - 71...17 Brou'en It 13... 8... 4... 1...12 25- 99...18 19- 66...17 Hull l3. 7 4. 2..14 20- 88...15 17- 81...16 Salford Swinton l5- 7.- 7.- 1.-10 18-- 74.-18 16-- 84..15 Hull K.R l5- 7.- B_. 0.-22 20-104...12 19-- 81.-14 Hallfax - l5- 6.- 8.- 1.-13 14-- 68-. 8 15-- 61.-13 Wakefield 15-. 5.- 9.- 1_.20 8-- 64._16 15-- 77.-11 'Leigh l5-. 5.- 9.- 1.- 9 14-- 60_.17 22-100.-11 Widnes 14... 4... 9... 1... 9 15- 63...20 28-124... 9 Runcorn.. 14.- 4.-10.- 0.-12 14-- 66.-21 24-114- 8 St. Helens 13.- 4.- 9.- 0.-20 15-- 85.-23 36-154.- 8 Eldtley l5- 3.-11.- 1..14 13-- 67_.21 24-114.- 7</p>
        <p>There is a lot of see-sawing for position going on among the top clubs. Oldham succumbed for the second time on their own ground, Broughton Rangers this time doing the trick. Bradford in the meantime were easily conquering St: Helens, and they once more lead the way in the tournament. Warrington's defeat has lost them a couple of places as well as points. Batley won a match on Saturday, and as it was at the expense of the cup-holders the fact is addi- tionally worth noting.</p>
        <p>Since writing the above notes in the earlier part of the week, the Warrington team for Saturday has been selected, and I find that changes have already taken place. J. Davies has returned, and supplants Dai Davies at half back, which on the form of the two men is only to be expected. Harris has also been relegated to the "A " team and Brooks placed on the wing. Since his injury, Harris has apparently lost much of his pace and scoring ability. and a change, temporary may be, was certainly required. Fish is in his usual place, and I hope he will to-day give a display more worthy of his reputation. In the forwards J. Swift jun., comes in, J. Jolley taking a well-earned rest.</p>
        <p>To tackle Broughton Rangers at Wheater's Field just now is a large order, but I shall not be surprised to see Warrington do better than last week. They often show their best form against the strongest opponents, and the Rangers at present are playing as well as any side. The return of Wilson has been like a draught of elixir to the team, and Warrington will have their work cut out to repeat last season's performance at Broughton.</p>
        <p>The prospective teams are: Warrington : Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Brooks, threequarters ; Hockenhall and J. Davies, half backs; Boardman, Shugars, Preston, Thomas, Belton, Cook, Naylor, and Swift, forwards.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers.---Poole, back; Hogg, Wilson, Harry, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; S. James and Horton, half backs ; Trotter, Scott, Oram, Winskill, Templeton, Ruddick, Whitehead, and Beet- ham, forwards.</p>
        <p>Last night Dickenson and Isherwood were found to be unable to play, and Harris and Duckworth come into the team.</p>
        <p>The " A " team had a regular field day on Saturday, and the weakness of the Salford reserve is a testimony to the paucity of talent possessed by the Royal Borough. Though short of J. Davies and Brooks, the Warrington Juniors ran up the tremendous score of 3 goals, ten tries to nil. R. Bate made a welcome re-appearance, and he and Duckworth proved that the old-time partnership was still effective.</p>
        <p>Wigan " A " at present head the Comlination table but the Warrington men hold the better position on matches played. If all goes well the meeting of the two teams at Wilderspool on February ISth should have an important bearing on the destination of the championship medals.</p>
