      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <p>1'</p>
        <p>Royal Beef for Warrington. Mr. H. Singleton has again purchased for sale in Warrington, the prize winner shown by the King at the Smithfield annual cattle show. Cab Accident. Considerable excitement was caused in the neigh-</p>
        <p>bou-rhood of the Central Station, on Saturday night, when, at about nine o'clock a horse attached to one of the cabs stationed at the Central Station bolted up Crown-street and down Orford-street. A tramcar was travelling along Et_uttermarket-street, and luckily the driver noticed the horse coming in his direction and pulled up sharply, thereby preventing what might have been a very serious accident. The cab just grazed the car and then overturned. The horse was slightly injured, and the cab very little damaged. A Dangerous Practice. Considerable danger arises from the moving of gas meters by inexperienced men, and the Corporation have consequently ordered that the position of meters shall not be altered without the knowledge and con- sent of the gas officials. The contravention of this bye-law led to the appearance in the Warrington police court yesterday of Alfred Hudson, 104, Buttermarket-street.—Mr. Stanley Wilson stated that on the 18th November, an intimation was conveyed to the gas works that there was a serious leakage of gas at 29, Hutme-street. A fitter happened to be on the premises and he was immediately sent to the house. Here he found that the leakage was due to a nail having been driven through the supply pipe, and the other pipes having been twisted by the moving of the meter. If the fitter had not been able to go down a serious explosion would no doubt have occurred. Defendant said he was doing some work at the house which necessitated the moving of the meter. He did not know he was doing wrong. Defendant was fined in the costs the magistrates intimating that future cases would be more severely dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <p>have been selected: Light; Short, Berry, D. Davies• and Harris • Bate and Duckworth; Harmer, Lunt, Morrison* Edmondson, and Coleman.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting matches of the season should be witnessed at Bradford on Jannary 2, when. the Northern Union " internatianal " comes off.</p>
        <p>Practically all the stars of the Union are billed to appear on one side or the other. A great honour has been done the Warrington Glab by the selection of no less than three players, and three more as reserves. Fish and Boardman will represent England, with, Hockenhall and Preston Reserves, while Thomas will play for the other nationalities, with Shugars as. reserve.</p>
        <p>The English side is unquestionably a magnificent fifteen and I doubt whether another team could be picked from either England, Scotland, or Ireland which could beat it. The mixed team is little inferior except perhaps at centre and half-back. Lomas and Marsden should make a remarkable com- bination behind English scrummage, and if there are. any Rugby Unionists present they should pick up a thing or two from these really great players.</p>
