      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Corporation Contracts Question.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held in the Bold-street schools on Tuesday evening, Mr. Podmore presiding over a fair attendance of delegates.</p>
        <p>CORPORATION CONTRACTS CLAUSES. THE couscm AND TILE PRINTING CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>Councillor STorr said there had been—iiii—iniormal meeting of the members of the sub-committee appointed by the Corporation to consider the question of the clauses regulating work in connection with Corporation contracts. It was decided to invite employers' and employes' organizations to provide evidence which would tisist the committee in coming to a decision upon the question. A letter was read from the Newton Branch of the Typographical Associa- tion asking permission to send a deputation to appear before the sub-committee and give evidence regarding the printing contract, and it was stated that in all probability the committee would be prepared to receive that and other deputations from trade union organiza- tions. The building industries federation had also expressed their willingness to co-operate in the matter. with regard to the printing I con- tract having gone to Messrs. Mackie and Co., that had given rise to a large amount of discussion, but he would point out that the contract was given under the old standing orders which had been a nuisance ever since they were first introduced. When that matter came before the Council he did not feel justified at that juncture in contesting the placing of the tender. What the Trades Council wanted in regardlto Corporation con- tracts was fair play—the adoption of such clauses as would make it absolutely impossible for Corporation work to he done under unfair conditions.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. PLUMB, of the railway servants, advocated the. immediate holding of a demonstration to show the Town Conncil they were prepared to enforce their- demands.</p>
        <p>_ His view was not, however, shared by others present at the meeting and the matter was allowed to drop.</p>
        <p>FREE MEALS FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL SAY THEY SHOULD BE PROVIDED.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the National Labour Repre- sentation Committee asking the Council to be repre- sented at a conference to be held early neKt year to discuss the question of the unemployed and also the question of the provision by public authorities of free meals for school children.</p>
        <p>Councillor STorr said he believed a good proportion of the money spent upon elementary education was wasted owing to the fact that many of the children were not physically tit, through lack of proper feeding, to be taught. It was compulsory that the children should attend school and, he contended, it should there- fore be made compulsory that the responsible authori- ties should see that the children were sufficiently fed to receive that education which the teachers were trying to give them. The unemployed question, continued Councillor STOTT, was one which should be considered seriously by "every working man. "Both political parties," he said, have proved their utter inability to deal with this ques- tion,and our onlyihope is in the Labour party." It was decided that Councillor STOTT should represent the Council at the conference. THE SHOPS SUNDAY CLOSING BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. PODMORE appealed for the support of the Council to the "Shops Sunday Closing Bill," to be introduced' in the House of Commons next session by Lord Avebury. He pointed out that in the hairdresser'a trade they had to face severe and unfair competition through shops being open on Sundays. The Council decided to support the Bill and the national petition which is being got up in favour of the passing of the Bill, and to forward copies of a resolu- tion to that effect to Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., the Home Office, and the Archbishop of Canterbury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-17_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</p>
        <p>MRS. BALLIN, THE GREAT AUTHORITY ON CHILDREN'S DISEASES, STRONGLY RECOMMENDS VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
