        <p>LOCAL NEWS. -- Christmas at the Workhouse. With the sanction of the Local Government Board the Christmas festivities will take place at the Work- house on Saturday next this year. The question of "Christmas beer" has not been discussed at a Board meeting, but we understand that the inmates will have the usual allowance. Hockey Dance. The annual dance in connection with the Stockton Heath Hockey Club was held in the Parr Hall last night and was in every way highly successful and enjoyable. About a hundsed couples were present and dancing was kept up from 8 o'clock until 1.30, The music was supplied by W. P. Bailey's (Liverpool) band. Christmas at the Ragged School. Arrangements are being made for the annual Christmas treat given to the children at the Ragged school for which donations of sweets, oranges, &amp;c., are earnestly desired. Lady Greenall has presented a Christmas tree and it now remains for some kindly disposed persons to provide the articles to be hung upon it. Bewsey Road Wesleyan Church. The annual Foreign Missionary Sermons will be preached at this church to-morrow by the Rev. W . Russell Maltby of Liverpool. A special afternoon meeting will be held at 230, when Alderman Bennett will preside and Mr. Maltby will give an address. The Rev. W. Russell Maltby is acknowledged 'as one of the most gifted of the younger ministers of Methodism and as a preacher of much power and attractiveness. Services of unusual interest are con- fidently inticipated. At each service hymn sheets will be provided. The evening selected for the annual missionary meeting proved to be rather un- suitable ; a special effort will therefore be made to. morrow on behalf of the Foreign Missionary Funds. Reclaiming the Waifs and Strays. The admirable work which is being done by Dr. Barnardo's Homes is so well known that it needs little in the way of commendation. In ' Warrington an active branch of the Young Helpers' League is doing much to enlist support in such a deserving cause. Last evening the members and a large number of the general public had the pleasure of hearing a lecture, on "Slum life in our cities" by , Miss E. M. Price. warden of the league, in the Co operative Hall, Sankey Street. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes presided. The lecture was illustrated with lantern views and proved highly interesting. Musical items were submitted.</p>
