        <p>workpeople are scattered to tae soar winds. This is a real ruined industry, and it has not been ruined by Free Trade, but by closing our markets against cheap sugar. This is a type of what is going on all over the country, and it is a foretaste of more trouble to come. Mr. C. H. Wilson, M.P., who has had fifty-four years' experience as a merchant and trader in Hull, says: "I did all in my power to oppose the Sugar Convention, because I believed it would bnormously increase the cost of sugar. It has already increased it by£B,ooo,ooo a year. This is a first result of Mr. Chamberlain's first attempt at fiscal reform, but it is a trifle compared with the ruinous effects of Protection and Retaliation." Mr. Winston Churchill, M.P., says: "The Sugar Con- vention is the most inexplicable act of commercial folly ever perpetrated by a British Government ; Bedlam and Colney Hatch toil after them in vain. But what a piece of luck for Free Trade that this working model of Retaliation should be exhibited in all its disa,strous futility on the eve of a general election."</p>
