        <p>INLAND REVENUE BUSY. Unlicensed Carriages and Servants. MR HEYL-Dil PROSECUTED AT DARESBURY A case of more than usual interest was heard at Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, when George Edward Heyl-Dia, Stretton, was summoned for keeping divers carriages requiring licences and not having licences for the same on August 19th. Mr. Cecil I Simpson, of the Solicitor's Department of the Inland Revenue, prosecuted aud Mr. Arther Browne (Warring- ton) appeared for the defendant. Mr. Simpson said that the officers of the Inland Revenue had seen two motor" cars and one four wheeled. carriage, belonging to the defendant, in use, whereas he had only a licence for one car. In December, 1903, defendant had registered two cars—E.D. 6 and E.D. 7 and only took out a license for one, although both had been seen in use. Several Inland Revenue officers were called, and stated , that they had seen the cars and carriage in use this I year. Mr. Browne saidlthat the carriage referred to was only bought late in the year and was not used until Mr. Heyl-Dia had purchased and broken in two Aberdeen- shire ponies. When it was seen out,it was only because the ponies were being tried by one of Mr. Heyl-Dia's men—and unknown to Mr. Heyl-Dia. As soon as the ponies were properly broken in—in September a liconce , was taken out. He contended that the licence was 1 'taken out as soon as it was really necessary. With re- gard to the motor cars it was true that they had only one licenee, but they had really only one car. The car E.D. 7 was only used for experimental purposes and , was not a complete car. In fact, Mr. Heyl-Dia had only had it for a few days during the whole year. It was at Coventry eight weeks and was in Manchester now. Mr. Heyl-Dia corroborated this statement. The magistrates decided against the defendant with reference to the motor cars and lined him 20s. costs, but declined to convict in respect to the pony carriage. WHAT CONSTITUTES A MALE SERVANT? AN IMPORTANT PROSECUTION. Mr. Heyl-Dia was then summoned for employing , three male servants without having taken out licences permitting him to do so. Mr. Simpson said the principal dispute between the revenue authorities and the defendant was that he declined to pay the excise licence, for what he (Mr. Simpson) submitted could not be termed anything else but a chauffeur. In December last the defendant obtained a motor car driver's licence, and a man in his employ, Charles Purslow, also applied fora driver's licence, both of which were granted. Up to December last the defendant paid for a, gardener,lbut refused to do so for the present year, and the allegations of the prose- cution were that the defendant employed not only Chas. Purslow as chauffeur but also two other men named Billington and Vernon as male servants at his residence at Stretton.</p>
