        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICTICOUNCIL. _ Officers' Duties. REPORT OF THE COMMITTEE, The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present : Messrs. Baxter (presiding), C. E. Linaker, C. E. Linaker inn., S. Woodward, T. Wright, C. Roylance, J. Mounfield, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, J. Stokes, J. L. Jones, A. Fryer, J. H. Davies, G. Pilling, G. Egerton- Warburton, J. Whitlow, J. B. Chadwick, A. J. Guest, H. Eyes, R. Billington,J. Smith,l3. Mawdsley, C. Horton, R. A. Chrimes, S. Horton, J. Rutter, G. F Ashton'(clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), Farring- ton (inspector of nuisances), Swinton and Diggle (surveyors), and Dr.lAdams (medical officer). THE WARRINGTON AND KNUTSFORD LIGHT RAILWAY. The Clerk read a letter from the Clerk to the County Council asking whether the Council had considered the Knutsford and Warrington Light Railway scheme and iif so whether they were in favour of it or n opposition to it. The Clerk pointed out that the railway would not touch any of the District Council roads. It was decided to refer the question to the Parlia- ment committee of the Council. . HALTON AND WESTON WATER SUPPLY. The agreement with the Runcorn Urban Council for the supply of water to the parishes of Halton and Weston, was placed before the meeting. Mr. Baxter said he was pleased that the question had at last been settled. The committee of the District Council had by its persistency obtained very fair and equitable terms. Mr. Linaker:proposed the acceptance of the agreement. Mr. Pilling seconded, and congratulated the Com- mittee upon the excellent terms made by them. The proposition was unanimously carried. OFFICERS' DUTIES. RECOMMENDATIONS OF THE COMMITTEE. The Council considered and accepted the recommenda- tions of the committee appointed to• consider the duties of surveyors and inspector of nuisancev. The committee recommended, (1) The highways in the parishes of Acton Grange, Daresbury, Keckwick, Moore, Newton- by-Daresbury, Preston-o'-th'-Hill, and Whitley Inferior, having a total length of 16 miles 7 furlongs, and 50 yards, be transferred from Mr. Swinton to Mr. Diggle ; (2) Throughout the District of the Council, except within the parish of Stockton Heath, all matters in connection with sewerage, other than constructive duties, be transferred from the Surveyors to the Inspector of Nuisances; (3) The duties of Inspector of Nuisances in the Parish of Stockton Heath, except matters in connection with disinfection, be transferred from Mr. Farrington to Mr. Swinton; ,4) All roadmen ' and highway petty cash accounts be paid by cheque , through the Clerk's office- (5) No work, for which a ' loan is required, be done by the Surveyors; (6) The Surveyors and Inspector and Nuisances keep diaries of the duties performed by them and such diaries be' placed before the Council once a month; (7) Post cards be provided for the roadmen to notify the Surveyor whenever their work takes them from one district to an- otheri(B) The foregoing system be in force for one year on trial from the 31st March, 1905. The report was adopted. TO ADVERTISE OUR PICTORIAL POSTCARDS We give a Nickel Silver. Watch Free, guaranteed to keep correct time for 3 years, a Case of Spoons or Melodeon. Write to-day, giving your -• ast-so. full name and address, and your promise , ---, to Sell or Return 72 Penny Pictorial Itii Postcards within 3 weeks from the time • c•-• you receive same. When sold, send us v:st , Ink the 6- you get for them, and we will Oititt forward you any of the above-mentioned \ : .... .•,, Presents. Write at once enclosing . - ...- 3 penny stamps for Postage, and yon will receive the pacl,et by return. Any person can sell the Packet in One hour.—THE IRISH PICTORIAL CARD CO., Princes Et., Cork.</p>
